WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.375 --> 00:00:04.337
( INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC PLAYING )

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.296
My father would drive me out.

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:14.968
He was-- incidentally,
my father was sometimes here,
sometimes not here.

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:18.847
He was really gone from the time
I was about four...

00:02:18.888 --> 00:02:23.143
but he'd show up from time
to time and take me out
for a ride...

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:25.270
in the car with the rumble seat.

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:30.608
But we'd drive out towards
Newark Airport, and it was
called Shantytown...

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:36.489
and there would be a couple
of acres of tents.

00:02:36.531 --> 00:02:39.409
And you'd see little bonfires...

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.539
and the- there were men
who had left their homes...

00:02:44.581 --> 00:02:47.208
or they couldn't support
their families.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:52.922
And they weren't vagrants,
because they lived there.

00:02:52.964 --> 00:02:57.343
And these were people
who didn't have any money.

00:02:58.761 --> 00:03:01.890
And they had shame.
There was a lot of shame!

00:03:01.931 --> 00:03:08.730
The um, the Depression,
if there's another word
to use to describe it...

00:03:08.771 --> 00:03:11.858
was--
it was fraught with shame.

00:03:15.153 --> 00:03:21.201
Times were so bad uh.. at uh..
for everybody. And so we were
no different.

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:27.540
Uh.. I remembered one time my
mother said to my father-- he
was a cab driver--

00:03:27.582 --> 00:03:32.378
"There's no milk on the table.
Why don't you go out and hack,
bring in some money?"

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:36.132
He says, "Nobody's getting in
taxis these days. They can't
afford it."

00:03:36.174 --> 00:03:40.803
She says,
"Go out. Hold up a bank!"

00:03:40.845 --> 00:03:46.684
My father went out; a- an
hour-and-a-half later, he threw
a quarter on the table.

00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:51.522
He said, "Here, buy some milk,"
and he started to cry.

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:58.571
And my mother would sit there
and look at him some day, uh..

00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:03.076
watching- watching him
smoke a cigar.

00:04:03.117 --> 00:04:07.789
She said, "Who do you think you
are with a cigar? Rockefeller?"

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:11.334
She came over
and she broke the cigar.

00:04:11.376 --> 00:04:13.878
From that point on,
my father smoked a pipe.

00:04:18.341 --> 00:04:22.178
All comedy comes out of tragedy.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:26.432
Which is, you know, it's a
shock! It's a uh.. a social
shock.

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:31.521
And it's a terrible thing
for people to just have the rug
pulled out.

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:40.071
But that's when human
creativity sprouts.

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:42.949
It's forced.

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:47.495
"Have to do something
about this!" Gotta eat!

00:04:47.537 --> 00:04:52.041
So where do we start""

00:04:52.083 --> 00:04:56.337
They used to say uh.., "My
father couldn't work."

00:04:56.379 --> 00:04:58.548
That was the-- they called that
stock lines.

00:04:58.589 --> 00:05:04.304
Everybody used the same line.
"He couldn't work. There was no
food in the house.

00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:09.475
We couldn't pay the rent. And
the landlord was going to
dispossess us,"

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:13.438
and then came the Depression.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:17.025
And people laughed.
It was created from that period.

00:05:17.066 --> 00:05:22.530
NARRATOR: New York, October,
1929. Everything was calm and
peaceful in this great city.

00:05:22.572 --> 00:05:25.616
Then suddenly the bottom
dropped out of Wall Street.

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:29.078
But were the brokers
discouraged? No!

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.998
They knew prosperity was just
around the corner.

00:05:31.998 --> 00:05:35.501
Some preferred white bread.
Some whole wheat.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:38.838
Others rye.
The birds had little choice.

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:43.426
I was born an orphan.

00:05:43.468 --> 00:05:45.136
I didn't have any home...

00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:50.767
so I was one of the kids who
grew up coming out
of absolute poverty.

00:05:50.808 --> 00:05:55.021
I didn't have any future. I
didn't know what I wanted to be.

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:59.150
So I started hitchhiking across
the country to see what would
happen.

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:01.861
This was in 1929.

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:06.157
In June, 1929.

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:10.703
And I hitchhiked up the coast
to Walla Walla, Washington...

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:15.458
and then I wanted across the
Rockies, and that was tough on
hitchhiking...

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:19.212
so I started becoming a hobo
on railroads.

00:06:19.212 --> 00:06:23.132
I rode freight trains across the
country, always kept going.

00:06:23.174 --> 00:06:26.969
I had no money whatsoever
with me. Maybe five dollars.

00:06:27.011 --> 00:06:31.057
And uh.. every time there
was a job offered, I took it...

00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:34.477
whether I knew what to do
with it or not.

00:06:34.519 --> 00:06:36.062
Two bucks a day for this.

00:06:36.104 --> 00:06:37.397
All I have to do is walk up and
down, turn the light on and off.

00:06:37.438 --> 00:06:39.273
This your regular job?

00:06:39.315 --> 00:06:41.567
No, no, I don't believe
in regular jobs. I do this
for a friend of mine.

00:06:41.609 --> 00:06:43.569
I sub for him on his night off.

00:06:43.611 --> 00:06:46.239
And all you gotta do is walk up
and down here?

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:47.740
That's all.

00:06:47.782 --> 00:06:49.534
Is it all right if I walk
up and down with ya?

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:51.994
Sure. It's a free country,
ain't it?

00:06:52.036 --> 00:06:53.746
I got to New York.

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:59.961
I was sleeping in the YMCA gym
in Brooklyn, and there was a job
open as a typist.

00:07:00.002 --> 00:07:06.551
And I was on Wall Street,
and I was working away at it
every day in August and July.

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:12.932
And then along came October.
And this was a great turning
point in my life.

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:14.517
On October 29th, 1929, the
Depression, the Great Depression
started.

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:29.615
And they couldn't keep up
with the way the markets were
falling, the prices changing.

00:07:29.615 --> 00:07:34.787
You couldn't sell your stock
even if you wanted to, and you
knew it was going down...

00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:39.041
'cause you couldn't the-- you
couldn't get the thing, the
message through.

00:07:39.083 --> 00:07:43.296
Everything was jammed. The uh..
the people in the office...

00:07:43.296 --> 00:07:45.798
at the National City Bank of New
York where I worked...

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:52.763
were all crying because they'd
all invested all of their
savings in a-- on Wall Street.

00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:56.184
Wherever you looked, boys and
girls were-- uh...

00:07:56.225 --> 00:08:00.646
men and women were wondering
what was ever going to happen
to them.

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:06.569
And I was wondering a little
about it, but I was wondering
that since the time I was born..

00:08:06.611 --> 00:08:09.280
so I had an advantage
over the rest of them.

00:08:09.322 --> 00:08:11.991
Narrator:
Contending with modern economic
difficulties...

00:08:12.033 --> 00:08:14.660
requires quite as high courage
and as much enterprise...

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:18.623
as was were displayed by men
and women of covered wagon
days.

00:08:22.001 --> 00:08:25.796
I was born in Brooklyn, New
York, on a boarding house
table...

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:32.803
and my f- father had come from
Glasgow, Scotland, was in the
First World War...

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:36.265
and he became a comic.

00:08:36.307 --> 00:08:41.812
And um, I stayed in uh...
in burlesque at the time.

00:08:41.854 --> 00:08:44.649
Uh.. the people wanted to laugh.

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:48.861
There was nothing to laugh
about, and uh.. they got
together and said,

00:08:48.861 --> 00:08:50.488
"Let's have a burlesque show."
Said, "What's that?"

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:55.701
"Just gags and- and uh.. dance
group and this and that."

00:08:57.036 --> 00:08:59.872
My mother brought me
to California.

00:08:59.872 --> 00:09:04.210
She uh.. in a broken down car
and uh.. she said...

00:09:05.836 --> 00:09:12.009
"I've got a feeling my son
wants to be something."

00:09:12.051 --> 00:09:16.013
So we traveled and we-- there
were no motels or anything.

00:09:16.055 --> 00:09:20.726
We slept on dirty, dirty
mattresses.

00:09:23.437 --> 00:09:26.232
It wasn't easy, and uh.. no
roads, no maps.

00:09:34.949 --> 00:09:38.077
"Are we on the way to
California?" "Ah, I think it's
down that way."

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:42.164
You know, that-- it was rough
on my mother.

00:09:44.292 --> 00:09:46.877
I remember being born.

00:09:46.919 --> 00:09:51.340
So, I have a total recall of all
of my life.

00:09:51.382 --> 00:09:57.138
When I was 19 years old, I went
to the Greyhound bus...

00:09:57.179 --> 00:10:01.434
to get on the bus, and looked
in the United Cigar Store
window...

00:10:01.475 --> 00:10:07.189
and there was a copy of a book
there called, "Grapes of Wrath"
by John Steinbeck.

00:10:07.231 --> 00:10:10.526
I bought "The Grapes of Wrath,"
got on the bus...

00:10:10.568 --> 00:10:15.740
and rode through the Dust Bowl
on Highway 66...

00:10:15.781 --> 00:10:18.492
reading about it in the book.
The book was something.

00:10:18.534 --> 00:10:23.706
And Steinbeck spoke to me. So
when I wrote "The Martian
Chronicles,"

00:10:23.748 --> 00:10:30.546
I borrowed the ideas
for "The Martian Chronicles"
from "The Grapes of Wrath."

00:10:30.588 --> 00:10:34.884
I remember trying to think about
how I could take my bicycle...

00:10:34.925 --> 00:10:37.887
and how I could put some wings
on the sides of it...

00:10:37.928 --> 00:10:41.057
and maybe get a little bit
of lift as I pedaled along.

00:10:41.098 --> 00:10:46.228
And of course, I built cockpits
out of uh.. orange crates...

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:49.690
and- and cans and things that
resembled a throttle...

00:10:49.732 --> 00:10:51.817
and here I'd never even been
in an airplane...

00:10:51.859 --> 00:10:57.239
but- but it was
uh.. a kinda make believe.

00:10:57.281 --> 00:11:03.663
And in those days you
listened to "The Shadow"
and "The Creaking Door."

00:11:03.704 --> 00:11:09.293
And you- you didn't have the
imagination stolen from you...

00:11:09.335 --> 00:11:13.923
by the visuals of television;
you had to imagine all these
things.

00:11:13.964 --> 00:11:20.346
The- the thing that uh.. was
amazing about the times that
we'll never see them again...

00:11:20.388 --> 00:11:25.309
was that people were, in my
opinion, heroically passive.

00:11:25.351 --> 00:11:27.937
They just took whatever life
handed them.

00:11:27.978 --> 00:11:32.817
They obeyed the law as much as
possible, except when it came to
feeding their families.

00:11:32.858 --> 00:11:38.155
Then they would steal or they
would do anything that they had
to do to survive.

00:11:40.866 --> 00:11:44.537
I remember stealing food
to bring home.

00:11:44.578 --> 00:11:47.456
I remember looking behind uh..

00:11:47.498 --> 00:11:51.711
the m- markets or garbage cans
looking for- for like-

00:11:51.752 --> 00:11:57.425
like head- head of lettuce that
still had some good leaves on.
Uh.. you know, uh...

00:11:57.466 --> 00:12:04.181
we did those things to stay
alive. And uhm.. my father took
off.

00:12:04.223 --> 00:12:09.979
My- my mother remarried and uh..
had a stepfather who was the
kind of--

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:15.735
had that kind of ethic that the
man worked, you know, the woman
cooked, the man worked.

00:12:15.776 --> 00:12:17.653
And, there was no work.

00:12:17.695 --> 00:12:24.201
There was no work, and that
impacted on him, and he turned
just vicious, um...

00:12:24.243 --> 00:12:25.453
suffering from that.

00:12:25.494 --> 00:12:29.331
So the whole family unit
suffered in so many ways.

00:12:31.917 --> 00:12:36.338
The Great Depression of the
1930s is not just another
commodity bubble...

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:40.801
or price bubble. It's- it--
the reason it was so deep
and lasted so long...

00:12:40.843 --> 00:12:44.430
is because there was a
convergence of so many things
happening.

00:12:44.472 --> 00:12:47.433
There was the- the great bubble
of stock prices, of course,
in the 1920s...

00:12:47.475 --> 00:12:49.518
and that's because of the stock
market crash.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:53.814
But we've seen stock market
crashes of kind of comparable
proportions...

00:12:53.856 --> 00:12:56.150
without the ensuing great
economic chaos...

00:12:56.192 --> 00:13:00.237
so there's something more and
deeper at work here in the
1930s.

00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:02.281
And uh.. to be honest about
it...

00:13:02.281 --> 00:13:07.703
economic historians do not agree
to this day about exactly what
happened.

00:13:07.745 --> 00:13:10.790
I remember when the banks closed
in '32, and- and uhm..

00:13:10.831 --> 00:13:14.460
my mother called my brother and
me in-- we were all playing in
the yard, and she said,

00:13:14.502 --> 00:13:19.465
"I have bad news." And I thought
maybe a cherished relative had
died or something.

00:13:19.507 --> 00:13:22.802
And- and she said, "The banks
have closed, and you will not
see the money...

00:13:22.843 --> 00:13:26.597
that you have in the bank
anymore, because we'll probably
never get it back."

00:13:26.639 --> 00:13:28.474
We didn't have Social Security.

00:13:28.474 --> 00:13:31.811
We didn't have a lotta the
things that people have today
to, you know...

00:13:31.852 --> 00:13:33.521
to guide them through rough
times.

00:13:33.562 --> 00:13:37.900
So it was uh.. I mean,
you were totally on your own.

00:13:37.942 --> 00:13:39.985
I was, guess about 11 year
old...

00:13:39.985 --> 00:13:44.532
1936 when lotta people came to
California from Oklahoma...

00:13:44.573 --> 00:13:46.992
in that, you know,
the Dust Bowl.

00:13:46.992 --> 00:13:49.703
But growing up in the area, uh..

00:13:49.745 --> 00:13:54.333
we like some of our friends,
we'd like to say, "We were poor,

00:13:54.333 --> 00:13:58.379
but we didn't know it," 'cause
everybody was poor.

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:02.258
I think maybe the Okies made it
because of sheer
determination...

00:14:02.299 --> 00:14:05.344
but if you didn't have any
money, you didn't have a car or-
uh..

00:14:05.386 --> 00:14:08.597
how we you gonna make that move?
It was too tough.

00:14:08.639 --> 00:14:13.394
Although I do know one family
who uh.. who made it very
successfully in Canada...

00:14:13.435 --> 00:14:17.940
and they told me stories of how
they piled I think five of six
kids...

00:14:17.982 --> 00:14:21.777
the mother and the father in the
car with all their early
belongings...

00:14:21.819 --> 00:14:27.533
with frying pans still hanging
from the sides of the car,
just like they Okies did.

00:14:27.575 --> 00:14:31.537
And they stopped off in little
places and eventually, uh..

00:14:31.579 --> 00:14:35.916
one son was deposited in Regina,
another one in Saskatoon,
another one in Calgary.

00:14:35.958 --> 00:14:39.420
Uh.. they each tried to make
their own way in various parts
of Canada.

00:14:39.461 --> 00:14:41.881
It was, you know, Charles
Dickens once wrote, said,

00:14:41.922 --> 00:14:46.343
"It was the best of times; it
was the worst of times." Believe
me, it was the worst of times.

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:53.058
People were walking around
without eating.

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:58.397
And there's no need to talk
history of what happened in
terms of the very famous...

00:14:58.439 --> 00:15:02.651
rich, who, you know, what
happened to them.

00:15:02.693 --> 00:15:07.323
But the- the real--the real--

00:15:07.364 --> 00:15:12.453
you felt like the country was
going down, you know?

00:15:20.544 --> 00:15:26.550
We lost our house in 1933 and
were thrown out on the street.

00:15:26.592 --> 00:15:28.427
And uh..

00:15:28.469 --> 00:15:33.807
it certainly is one of
the most awful moments of my
life ever.

00:15:33.849 --> 00:15:38.604
And my father
was not a worldly man.

00:15:38.646 --> 00:15:42.274
Who, when everybody was making
money in the stock market, and
everybody was--

00:15:42.316 --> 00:15:47.196
and people were saying, "Gee,
Joe, what-- where-- what do you
do with your money?" "I got it."

00:15:47.237 --> 00:15:51.867
He was keeping it in the house,
hidden. And they said, "You
know, that's crazy.

00:15:51.909 --> 00:15:58.499
Your house can burn down. Invest
it." Well, he didn't know, so he
invested in 1928.

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:02.670
I think he invested in Electric
Bond and Share and Anaconda
Copper.

00:16:02.711 --> 00:16:08.133
And of course, he bought on the
margin, as everybody did.

00:16:08.175 --> 00:16:11.470
Uh.. I mean, that's what they--
they thought that was the way to
do it...

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:15.307
and they were encouraged to do
it by the brokers.

00:16:15.349 --> 00:16:19.728
And uh.. when everything
collapsed, we lost everything,

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:25.192
and then we uh.. my father
was never the same man.

00:16:25.234 --> 00:16:29.655
And we were having lunch, and a
man that they knew...

00:16:29.655 --> 00:16:35.786
the man at the bank who handled
the mortgages came in, and he
said,

00:16:35.828 --> 00:16:37.746
"What're you people doing here?"

00:16:37.788 --> 00:16:41.041
And my mother said-- I'm not
going to mention his name...

00:16:41.083 --> 00:16:47.214
because he still has family in
Wilkesboro-- and my mother said,
"Mr. X, we're having our lunch.

00:16:47.256 --> 00:16:49.717
Would you like to sit down and
have something to eat?"

00:16:49.758 --> 00:16:54.596
And he said, "You can't have
lunch here. This is not your
house. This is my house!"

00:16:54.638 --> 00:16:58.434
My father just-- by this time,
was just gone.

00:16:58.475 --> 00:17:03.147
And my mother said-- my mother's
a very strong woman, very brave
woman-- and she said, uh..

00:17:03.188 --> 00:17:09.903
she said, "Well, Mr. X, uh..
it's our house. We live here.
It's our house."

00:17:09.945 --> 00:17:15.868
He said, "Look, it's not your
house!" And he went-- And he
spit on the rug.

00:17:15.868 --> 00:17:20.539
And with that, I mean, everybody
just coll-- the family just
collapsed...

00:17:20.539 --> 00:17:23.625
and we were herded out of the
house. Our f- furniture was
taken out.

00:17:23.667 --> 00:17:25.711
Everything was taken out of the
house and put on the street.

00:17:25.711 --> 00:17:28.630
Most of which disappeared
shortly thereafter.

00:17:28.672 --> 00:17:34.053
We went-- my mother had a
sister, who was married
to a- a very decent man...

00:17:34.094 --> 00:17:38.057
who was an old socialist and
didn't believe in the stock
market or, you know...

00:17:38.098 --> 00:17:39.933
profiting by anybody else's
labor.

00:17:39.975 --> 00:17:43.562
He had a small furniture store
in West Pittston,
Pennsylvania...

00:17:43.604 --> 00:17:48.317
and uh.. we went to live
with them.

00:17:51.403 --> 00:17:55.324
I grew up in the Depression,
but when you have nothing to
contrast it with...

00:17:55.365 --> 00:17:57.242
and all your neighbors are
living the same way...

00:17:57.284 --> 00:18:00.496
you don't know you're in a Great
Depression, or even a mild
Depression.

00:18:00.537 --> 00:18:05.375
But one of our saving graces--
and I think this is true of many
people around that time...

00:18:05.417 --> 00:18:11.924
in a rural or a semirural area,
we had a very big garden which
was twice as big as our house...

00:18:11.924 --> 00:18:16.095
and we raised a whole lot
of uh.. vegetables.

00:18:16.136 --> 00:18:20.682
And so all summer we're living
out of the garden, and uh..

00:18:20.724 --> 00:18:23.560
in a sense,
food is free in that way.

00:18:23.602 --> 00:18:26.105
Uh.. we raised chickens. We had
uh.. 25 chickens.

00:18:26.146 --> 00:18:29.900
We- we didn't kill them very
often, because then you don't
have as many eggs.

00:18:29.942 --> 00:18:34.780
And I-- one of my chores was
to gather all the eggs,
which happened daily.

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:39.785
And the family couldn't eat more
than six or eight, ten eggs a
day--

00:18:39.827 --> 00:18:41.829
and so we would give them
to neighbors.

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:44.456
And it wasn't like, "I'll give
you this if you give me that."

00:18:44.456 --> 00:18:46.083
It was just,
"Here, take some eggs."

00:18:46.125 --> 00:18:50.212
And at other times, they'd bring
you milk, if they had a cow.

00:18:50.254 --> 00:18:53.966
That's how these uh.. programs
work in the third world
countries...

00:18:53.966 --> 00:18:57.636
where they give a family a goat,
and that's not just-- they have
milk...

00:18:57.678 --> 00:19:00.013
they get enough milk
for neighbors.

00:19:00.055 --> 00:19:04.518
And in that way, they can barter
for other things, and sometimes
even start a small business...

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:06.645
like a little dairy
or something.

00:19:06.645 --> 00:19:10.816
is what...and challenges

00:19:10.816 --> 00:19:14.987
I think this work
ethic produced.

00:19:14.987 --> 00:19:19.324
You had to move on and you had
to.. you had to be creative.

00:19:19.324 --> 00:19:22.119
You had to have a creative mind.

00:19:24.746 --> 00:19:28.709
Well, things looks pretty
tough...

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:33.130
but it ain't going to be this
way all the time.

00:19:38.552 --> 00:19:42.514
The Depression was a
depression...

00:19:42.514 --> 00:19:47.227
in a physical, economic sense.

00:19:47.269 --> 00:19:53.525
But in the social, and the
cultural, and the spiritual
sense, it was a stimulation.

00:19:55.068 --> 00:20:00.532
Struggle and strife and trauma
and tragedy uh..

00:20:00.574 --> 00:20:03.076
are great sources of our
greatest strengths...

00:20:03.118 --> 00:20:07.080
and one way is that in current
evolutionary thinking...

00:20:07.122 --> 00:20:12.878
our basic social tendencies to-
to cooperate, to trust, to uh..

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:19.259
to sacrifice and to give are-
are a product of our collective
sociality...

00:20:19.301 --> 00:20:24.056
responding to struggle, and- and
threat, right?

00:20:24.097 --> 00:20:28.477
And so when we enter into
historical times where there is
resource deprivation...

00:20:28.518 --> 00:20:32.731
or there are threats, it's-- it
turns that on, and people feel
routinely...

00:20:32.773 --> 00:20:37.569
you know, in the Great
Depression, or during wartime,
or during, you know, uh..

00:20:37.611 --> 00:20:40.989
economic troubles, people feel
immediately this bonding...

00:20:41.031 --> 00:20:44.409
tendency that's a great product
of times of-of strife.

00:20:44.409 --> 00:20:50.040
Even now, we are not so much
divided as we are disconnected.

00:20:50.082 --> 00:20:53.585
If we just could-- power comes
from connection.

00:20:53.585 --> 00:20:58.048
Plug it in
and you receive power.

00:20:58.090 --> 00:21:02.052
So I enjoy connecting.

00:21:02.094 --> 00:21:06.056
I have 106 godchildren...

00:21:06.098 --> 00:21:11.144
including the President.

00:21:11.186 --> 00:21:13.522
He is one of my godchildren.

00:21:13.563 --> 00:21:16.858
106! You know what?

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:20.779
Because I listen. I pray
with them. I talk with them.

00:21:20.821 --> 00:21:25.951
I give them directions. We got
to go back to being parents.

00:21:25.993 --> 00:21:30.664
My daddy just didn't-- and mama
didn't raise me.

00:21:30.706 --> 00:21:34.167
Everybody in my community
raised me!

00:21:34.209 --> 00:21:38.338
"I remember you."

00:21:38.380 --> 00:21:41.341
You know, but in those days,
people would help each other.

00:21:41.383 --> 00:21:44.636
You know, if this person had
something you didn't have, you
know, you--

00:21:44.678 --> 00:21:47.472
you'd trade it for it,
or give it to, you know.

00:21:47.514 --> 00:21:49.975
And that really brings out
the best in people.

00:21:49.975 --> 00:21:56.189
I opened my first official gym
in Oakland in 1936.

00:21:56.231 --> 00:21:59.026
See I was the first one to have
women working out with the
weights.

00:21:59.067 --> 00:22:01.486
I was the first one to have old
people working out with the
weights.

00:22:01.528 --> 00:22:03.989
I was the first one to have
athletes working out with the
weights.

00:22:03.989 --> 00:22:07.701
The word got around, "That Jack
LaLanne, he's a nut! He's a
crackpot! You know?

00:22:07.743 --> 00:22:10.829
Got women working out with the
weights. Got athletes working
out with them.

00:22:10.829 --> 00:22:14.041
The athletes they'll get
muscle-bound. The old guys will
grab a heart attack!"

00:22:14.082 --> 00:22:17.294
You can't believe what I went
through! Boy, it was tough!

00:22:17.336 --> 00:22:22.007
Not only to open the first gym,
and everything, but then going
through the Depression.

00:22:22.007 --> 00:22:26.219
So people had no money, but
that's how I started.

00:22:26.261 --> 00:22:30.432
See, I wasn't thinking
of Depression, I was thinking
of helping people.

00:22:36.355 --> 00:22:39.691
Up, down, up, down...

00:22:39.691 --> 00:22:43.487
is this a racket, I ask you?

00:22:43.528 --> 00:22:48.075
Buy low, sell high,
buy low, sell high.

00:22:51.244 --> 00:22:56.792
I got a job in 1929 as I just
graduated from high school...

00:22:56.833 --> 00:23:00.879
as a messenger at Saloman
Brothers and Hutzler.

00:23:00.921 --> 00:23:05.550
I was one of the few people who
got out of Wall Street in
time...

00:23:05.550 --> 00:23:10.764
because by September 1st,
I had to go to college.

00:23:10.806 --> 00:23:15.143
So I got out of Wall Street and
was out of it for four years.

00:23:15.185 --> 00:23:20.565
My f- father was a uh..
insurance broker and agent...

00:23:20.607 --> 00:23:27.406
and had a small business with
very few employees and one day,
he came home.

00:23:27.447 --> 00:23:31.535
We sat down at dinner and he
said, "I just had to fire
someone...

00:23:31.576 --> 00:23:37.749
and it's really upset me
terribly." And at that point,
he broke into tears.

00:23:37.791 --> 00:23:41.795
I had never seen my father cry.

00:23:41.837 --> 00:23:45.674
Uh.. you know, in those days,
men didn't cry, you know.

00:23:45.715 --> 00:23:50.929
You weren't supposed to show
your feeling or what-- all the
nonsense that prevailed.

00:23:50.971 --> 00:23:53.849
But I've never forgotten that.

00:23:53.890 --> 00:23:59.271
I mean, it upset him terribly
and uh.. that he had to fire...

00:23:59.312 --> 00:24:05.569
some-- this man, 'cause-- and he
had to, because he didn't have
the income uh...

00:24:05.610 --> 00:24:07.779
you know, to- to pay the salary.

00:24:07.779 --> 00:24:13.577
I- it was just-- or else he
couldn't've brought home enough
money to feed us.

00:24:16.496 --> 00:24:22.252
In 1945 in November, I came back
from the war and I was in my Air
Force uniform.

00:24:22.294 --> 00:24:25.338
Still, I went back home, see my
mother and father.

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:29.926
We were driving around and I
suddenly-- the look in the
corner, the bank...

00:24:29.968 --> 00:24:35.307
and there I see Mr. X. And I
said, "Hey, hold it up for a
minute, will ya?"

00:24:35.348 --> 00:24:39.227
I was 23 years old and I was
just in great shape.

00:24:39.269 --> 00:24:42.022
And I walk into the bank and I
said, "Mr. X, do you remember
me?"

00:24:42.063 --> 00:24:45.609
and he said, "No, no. What is
it?" he said, "I'm busy."

00:24:45.650 --> 00:24:49.321
And I said, "Oh, just this--
and I spit.

00:24:49.362 --> 00:24:55.911
And he stood up, and I hit him,
and it was in-- crazy, it was
just one of those crazy moments.

00:24:55.952 --> 00:25:00.332
And I looked around and I was
smiling, and I looked and
everybody was like this.

00:25:00.332 --> 00:25:03.335
Nobody moved, nobody stopped me.
He was on the floor.

00:25:03.376 --> 00:25:08.590
I said, "That's what you did to
my mother and father."

00:25:08.632 --> 00:25:14.763
It was the most satisfying day
of my life, ever, ever.
Still to this day.

00:25:16.306 --> 00:25:19.100
There was a- a guy down the
street who had a candy store.

00:25:19.142 --> 00:25:22.938
The candy store was always the
center of life and his name
was Joe.

00:25:22.979 --> 00:25:27.567
He was a very good looking guy.
Always had a smile on his face.

00:25:27.609 --> 00:25:29.653
He wore nice clothes.

00:25:29.694 --> 00:25:35.909
And the nice thing about Joe
was, i- if you-- those days the
Daily News was two cents...

00:25:35.951 --> 00:25:39.371
and on a-- I think on Sunday it
was a nickel or whatever it was.

00:25:39.412 --> 00:25:44.501
And uh.. you came in on Saturday
and you got the Brooklyn
section...

00:25:44.543 --> 00:25:49.214
but you didn't have to pay for
the-- for the paper until you
got there on Sunday.

00:25:49.214 --> 00:25:51.925
And Joe would always let you
have the Brooklyn section...

00:25:51.967 --> 00:25:54.010
assuming that you would get the
rest of the paper on Sunday...

00:25:54.052 --> 00:25:56.054
and pay the rest of the money.

00:25:56.096 --> 00:25:59.349
So he was like, I don't know,
sweetest man in the world.

00:25:59.391 --> 00:26:04.646
One day we woke up, uh.. the
store was closed. What happened?

00:26:04.688 --> 00:26:08.692
Joe hung himself.

00:26:08.733 --> 00:26:13.238
We always-- I mean, really, I
mean it- it still comes back,
this "why?"

00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:17.450
I don't know. Couldn't pay the
rent, whatever it was.

00:26:17.492 --> 00:26:23.707
Uh.. those were some of the uh..
things that uh.. happened...

00:26:23.748 --> 00:26:29.588
I think uh.. to so many
people at that time.
Everybody had a story.

00:26:35.427 --> 00:26:39.431
From the time the United States
entered the era of the
Industrial Revolution...

00:26:39.431 --> 00:26:44.352
the early 19th Century, there
were periodic gigantic swings in
the business cycle.

00:26:44.394 --> 00:26:46.688
And what were called,
commonly, depressions.

00:26:46.730 --> 00:26:51.818
Uh.. in the 1830s, the 1850s,
the 1870s, the 1890s, a pretty
sharp on in 1921...

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:53.945
although it was quite brief.

00:26:53.987 --> 00:26:58.700
So the- the Great Depression
of the 1930s is not all that
unfamiliar an episode...

00:26:58.742 --> 00:27:02.412
except it's grander in scale,
and lasts longer than the
previous ones.

00:27:02.454 --> 00:27:05.248
And there has been nothing
comparable to it since!

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:08.627
You know, it was just an abysmal
event. It was essentially a lost
decade.

00:27:08.627 --> 00:27:13.423
I mean, there's a lotta workers
uh.. in their prime working
years that, you know, lost 10...

00:27:13.465 --> 00:27:17.260
12, 15 years uh.. of a work
history because of that.

00:27:17.302 --> 00:27:19.346
Uhm.. you know, it's almost like
a murder mystery.

00:27:19.387 --> 00:27:22.641
You know, we all have our
favorite whodunits um..
and--

00:27:22.641 --> 00:27:26.227
and what the Great Depression's
really about, you know,
who killed the economy?

00:27:26.269 --> 00:27:30.815
There was a whole generation
of great economists...

00:27:30.857 --> 00:27:36.863
who were brought into the
economic profession precisely...

00:27:36.905 --> 00:27:43.578
because they perceived that
something had gone very wrong
with our economic system...

00:27:43.620 --> 00:27:47.666
and they wanted to understand
what had gone wrong, and
furthermore...

00:27:47.707 --> 00:27:51.503
try to figure out how to fix
it...

00:27:51.544 --> 00:27:54.547
so that sort of thing
wouldn't happen again.

00:27:54.589 --> 00:27:59.636
And the key to preventing a
Great Depression is making your
financial institutions uh...

00:27:59.678 --> 00:28:03.264
fireproof, if you like.
Now it used to be we built our
cities entirely of wood.

00:28:03.306 --> 00:28:06.184
We crowded everybody together,
and you could have things like
the Great Fire of London...

00:28:06.184 --> 00:28:11.231
at the end of the 17th Century,
devastates a huge amount of the
city, and a lot of people...

00:28:11.272 --> 00:28:15.193
you know, lose- lose their
belongings and- and many of them
lose their lives.

00:28:15.193 --> 00:28:17.529
So you wanna build something
much more like a modern city...

00:28:17.529 --> 00:28:20.281
which has different kinds of
structures, more firebreaks...

00:28:20.323 --> 00:28:23.410
more firehouses, more fire
engines, uh...

00:28:23.451 --> 00:28:26.079
and that's what we wanna do
with our financial system also.

00:28:26.121 --> 00:28:31.209
There is no natural process
which updates our regulations.

00:28:31.209 --> 00:28:34.921
Uh.. historically, something
goes badly wrong...

00:28:34.963 --> 00:28:39.509
and then we recognize that
regulation g- got behind.

00:28:39.551 --> 00:28:42.971
You know ,when we have these-
these times of crisis...

00:28:43.012 --> 00:28:47.892
you know, it's particularly
important to think about all of
these different dimensions.

00:28:47.892 --> 00:28:52.772
And I think that most of the
economic policy debates are
focused on...

00:28:52.814 --> 00:28:57.736
how do you deal with regulating
financial markets and so forth.

00:28:57.736 --> 00:29:01.948
And that's very important,
but you need to go beyond that
uh...

00:29:01.990 --> 00:29:08.580
to, how do you have a more
durable society in which when
crises do happen...

00:29:08.621 --> 00:29:13.543
you have a uh.. better safety
net for people's lives.

00:29:13.585 --> 00:29:18.423
I- if you're gonna function,
you have to be certain
about some things.

00:29:18.423 --> 00:29:22.677
You have to be pretty certain
about who you trust; who loves
you;

00:29:22.719 --> 00:29:26.931
where the next meal's gonna come
from and what you're going to do
if it rains.

00:29:26.931 --> 00:29:29.184
You have to have that stuff
figured out...

00:29:29.225 --> 00:29:35.982
so that you can take risks and
grow and all the other things
that are so much fun to do.

00:29:36.024 --> 00:29:43.156
Now when that stuff gets
disturbed, it's physically and
emotionally terrible.

00:29:46.951 --> 00:29:50.663
There's a wonderful study that
research teams went around the
country...

00:29:50.705 --> 00:29:53.958
doing these in-depth interviews,
and they were trying to discover
all kinds of things...

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:56.669
about how the Depression had
affected people psychologically.

00:29:56.711 --> 00:30:01.132
There was one consistent finding
that they found in case after
case after case...

00:30:01.132 --> 00:30:05.220
that the unemployed man felt
personally guilty, and
ashamed...

00:30:05.261 --> 00:30:10.600
and mortified, embarrassed,
humbled, that he had been put
in this situation.

00:30:10.642 --> 00:30:14.771
Now we can look back
historically, and think, "Well,
wait a minute...

00:30:14.813 --> 00:30:18.149
this unemployed man we're
talking about here should have
looked around him...

00:30:18.149 --> 00:30:22.278
and seen there were 12,999,999
other people unemployed...

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:26.699
and it wasn't that he had
suddenly personally failed,
or lost his moral fiber.

00:30:26.741 --> 00:30:30.620
But the reflex of all kinds of
unemployed people...

00:30:30.662 --> 00:30:35.542
was to blame themselves for
their situation and not to blame
"the system."

00:30:35.583 --> 00:30:39.462
This was an impediment to a
collective political action.

00:30:39.504 --> 00:30:44.217
It was something Franklin
Roosevelt remarked repeatedly
to his campaign entourage...

00:30:44.259 --> 00:30:46.845
during the presidential
campaign of 1932.

00:30:46.886 --> 00:30:50.682
He said, "This is the worst
crisis this country has faced
since the Civil War...

00:30:50.682 --> 00:30:53.226
and why aren't people in the
streets? Why aren't they
rioting?

00:30:53.268 --> 00:30:57.230
Why aren't they demanding a
political response to this?"

00:31:01.192 --> 00:31:06.698
In contradiction to the popular
notion that the population
of America...

00:31:06.739 --> 00:31:10.368
was passive for the most
part during the worse days...

00:31:10.368 --> 00:31:13.663
of the Great Depression.
The opposite was true.

00:31:13.705 --> 00:31:17.208
In Chicago, for example,
among African-Americans...

00:31:17.208 --> 00:31:20.879
it was not uncommon in the first
full year of the Depression...

00:31:20.879 --> 00:31:26.551
1930, to find hundreds of black
men assembling in Washington
Park...

00:31:26.593 --> 00:31:31.389
on the South Side discussing
their plight and what they
should do about it.

00:31:31.389 --> 00:31:37.604
In August of 1931, over 1,000
people-- maybe 5,000--

00:31:37.645 --> 00:31:41.107
marched on a home where a widow
was about to be set out...

00:31:41.149 --> 00:31:44.277
by the police
and the sheriff's bailiffs.

00:31:44.319 --> 00:31:50.158
And those thousands of people
prevented her furnishings from
being put on the street.

00:31:50.199 --> 00:31:54.621
Unfortunately, uh..
there was a scuffle.

00:31:54.662 --> 00:31:58.875
The police drew their weapons,
and killed three of the
demonstrators.

00:31:58.917 --> 00:32:03.671
Whites joined blacks in a
massive march down South State
Street...

00:32:03.713 --> 00:32:08.259
that had over 30,000 marchers
parade past the funeral home...

00:32:08.259 --> 00:32:12.013
where the bodies of the three
deceased lay.

00:32:12.055 --> 00:32:15.141
You tie that in,
interracial marches...

00:32:15.183 --> 00:32:18.811
with the mid-1930s efforts
of labor unions...

00:32:18.853 --> 00:32:25.401
who were organizing in the mass
production industries, and
you've got a- a blending of uh..

00:32:25.443 --> 00:32:30.698
interests, and the overcoming--
temporarily at least-- of uh..

00:32:30.740 --> 00:32:33.618
past racial tensions.

00:32:36.079 --> 00:32:41.167
In the '30s, and this sort of
sets the stage in the north...

00:32:41.209 --> 00:32:45.797
for Dr. King being able
to be successful.

00:32:45.797 --> 00:32:47.632
We created, in a sense...

00:32:47.632 --> 00:32:53.137
parallel institutions, parallel
social institutions...

00:32:53.137 --> 00:32:56.474
political institutions,
economic institutions...

00:32:56.474 --> 00:33:00.770
cultural institutions, which
gave those of us who were
young...

00:33:00.812 --> 00:33:02.730
a feeling that we could
have fun...

00:33:02.772 --> 00:33:06.818
and that if we were serious
about the future, we'd go to
school.

00:33:06.818 --> 00:33:11.990
Uh.. we- we-- though we were
poor, we were not
poverty-stricken.

00:33:11.990 --> 00:33:15.868
And there's a difference. One is
a condition in which you happen
to be...

00:33:15.910 --> 00:33:20.623
one is an attitude that one day
things are going to change...

00:33:20.665 --> 00:33:27.005
and you're going to be the
beneficiary, if you just
continue, stay out of trouble,

00:33:27.005 --> 00:33:33.678
and get the kind of
qualifications-- education and
otherwise--

00:33:33.678 --> 00:33:38.307
that will be needed to-
to change your position.

00:33:38.349 --> 00:33:45.148
So most of us of that generation
at that time, did that.

00:33:47.942 --> 00:33:51.821
I was born in a house close to
the hospital. Segregated
hospitals.

00:33:51.863 --> 00:33:57.035
Uh.. segregated graveyards.
So from womb to tomb we faced
segregation.

00:33:57.076 --> 00:34:00.913
And that meant not only
separation, it also meant
restrictions...

00:34:00.955 --> 00:34:06.210
of access to education, and
healthcare, and jobs, and
development.

00:34:06.252 --> 00:34:09.005
So we lived in the shadows
of the Great Depression.

00:34:09.047 --> 00:34:14.594
So most blacks during that
period either knew living
grandparents being in slavery.

00:34:14.635 --> 00:34:19.891
Or there were many ex-slaves who
were still alive.

00:34:19.932 --> 00:34:21.851
So the least educated...

00:34:21.893 --> 00:34:27.065
the most vulnerable uh.. had--
took the greatest hit.

00:34:27.065 --> 00:34:29.901
And there's a-- it's an
inter-generational continuity.

00:34:29.901 --> 00:34:31.903
People who try to slice
and dice...

00:34:31.903 --> 00:34:36.908
history miss the fact that God
has not missed one day...

00:34:36.949 --> 00:34:41.204
since he started making days.
They're all connected.

00:34:47.168 --> 00:34:51.255
So much of the art of the '30s,
literature and visual art is...

00:34:51.297 --> 00:34:57.345
is rooted in an interest in
understanding what's happening
during the Depression.

00:34:57.386 --> 00:35:02.683
And particularly, you know, at
the outset of it when 25 percent
of Americans were unemployed.

00:35:02.725 --> 00:35:05.269
And this is, you know,
this is a huge number...

00:35:05.311 --> 00:35:08.481
and had a devastating impact
on people's lives.

00:35:08.523 --> 00:35:12.735
And artists were responding to
that impact uh..

00:35:12.777 --> 00:35:16.489
even the artists who were
carried over from the 1920s...

00:35:16.531 --> 00:35:19.367
like Ernest Hemingway may be the
best example of this.

00:35:19.408 --> 00:35:26.207
Hemingway was writing about
individuals during the 1920s and
the problems of individuals.

00:35:26.249 --> 00:35:31.462
In the 1930s, he is-- continues
to write these kinds of
stories...

00:35:31.462 --> 00:35:36.134
but then he writes a story about
the Spanish Civil War, "For Whom
the Bell Tolls."

00:35:36.175 --> 00:35:39.095
And he writes, "To Have and Have
Not," in which--

00:35:39.137 --> 00:35:43.891
in which the- the main character
concludes after, you know...

00:35:43.933 --> 00:35:50.022
terrible uh.. terrible events
during the novel, "A man alone
doesn't have a chance."

00:35:50.064 --> 00:35:56.070
So there's sense of the
collective, the need for other
people to depend upon...

00:35:56.112 --> 00:36:01.701
on their fellows is something
that comes up uh.. in- in that
literature...

00:36:01.742 --> 00:36:06.205
even in the literature uh.. of
people like Hemingway who came
in from the '20s.

00:36:06.247 --> 00:36:11.878
It's very obvious in literature
of Steinbeck, of course, who's,
you know maybe the preeminent...

00:36:11.919 --> 00:36:16.674
writer, or the one who
characterizes and in some
ways...

00:36:16.716 --> 00:36:20.636
caricatures the whole culture
of the '30s.

00:36:27.268 --> 00:36:32.440
One of the few song of that time
that really, at least the
Broadway songs, that- that...

00:36:32.481 --> 00:36:38.070
really hits the Depression full
on, I mean, talks about it in no
uncertain terms...

00:36:38.112 --> 00:36:41.657
"Brother, Can You Spare a Dime?"
is a very, very powerful song...

00:36:41.699 --> 00:36:44.660
written by "Yip" Harburg
and Jay Gorney.

00:36:44.702 --> 00:36:50.249
Both were card-carrying
Communists. I don't say that
disparagingly. It's just a fact.

00:36:50.291 --> 00:36:54.170
But they were in Central Park
trying to think of a topic for a
song...

00:36:54.212 --> 00:36:59.884
and they ran across a man... or
obviously a Harvard educated, an
Ivy League man...

00:36:59.926 --> 00:37:04.639
but he was down on his luck. He
was wearing evening clothes, and
he came up to them--

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:08.476
up to them and surprised them
by saying, "Brother, can you
spare a dime?"

00:37:08.517 --> 00:37:11.437
And they thought, "That's-
that's the great title." That's
the way the story goes.

00:37:11.479 --> 00:37:16.067
But it's a good song in many
ways. Great words, and it refers
back of course to World War I...

00:37:16.067 --> 00:37:19.904
it's all about a guy who was a
soldier in World War I, and you
know...

00:37:19.904 --> 00:37:23.532
the American government uh..
really did the dirty on--

00:37:23.574 --> 00:37:26.494
on the soldiers, the Dough Boys
who'd fought in World War I.

00:37:26.535 --> 00:37:29.038
They promised them a bonus at
the end of the war that they
were going to get his money...

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:30.873
and they never gave it to them.

00:37:30.915 --> 00:37:34.210
And of course, in the 1930s,
when Hoover was still
President...

00:37:34.252 --> 00:37:38.714
we have the famous Bonus Army
marching right the way across
the country...

00:37:38.756 --> 00:37:41.175
and setting up their tents
outside the White House.

00:37:41.217 --> 00:37:44.762
And the Army actually being
brought in to drive them out...

00:37:44.804 --> 00:37:48.182
I mean, physically, violently
dri- drive them out.

00:37:48.224 --> 00:37:51.978
But this song directly talks
about those soldiers.

00:37:52.019 --> 00:37:57.942
You know, "Ones in khaki suits.
Gee, we look swell, full of that
Yankee Doodle Dum.

00:37:57.942 --> 00:38:00.945
Half a million boots went
slogging through Hell.

00:38:00.945 --> 00:38:03.489
And I was the kid with the
drum." I like that line.

00:38:03.531 --> 00:38:08.244
"Say, don't you remember? They
called me 'Al.'" I mean, that's
a great detail...

00:38:08.286 --> 00:38:11.455
"They called me 'Al.'
It was 'Al' all the time.

00:38:11.455 --> 00:38:15.084
Say, don't you remember?
I'm your pal. Brother, can you
spare a dime?"

00:38:15.126 --> 00:38:20.298
Very powerful song to a minor
key, Eastern European, Jewish
melody.

00:38:20.298 --> 00:38:26.178
I mean, very powerful stuff
indeed! But of course, uh..
Harburg went on to write uh..

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:29.974
a piece of, call it fluff, but
certainly an escapist song...

00:38:30.016 --> 00:38:35.187
"Somewhere Over the Rainbow," so
uh.. you know, like great--

00:38:35.229 --> 00:38:38.316
all great song writers, they can
write all kinds of material.

00:38:38.357 --> 00:38:42.111
"Oh, we ain't got a barrel
of money.

00:38:42.153 --> 00:38:44.905
Maybe we're ragged and funny.

00:38:44.947 --> 00:38:50.911
But we'll travel along, singing
a song, side-by-side."

00:38:50.953 --> 00:38:56.751
And you know, people went on to
sing that song v-- in a very
jolly fashion.

00:38:58.878 --> 00:39:04.425
But actually the words are- are
very touching, and in fact,
darkly touching.

00:39:04.467 --> 00:39:07.178
And the- the verse...

00:39:07.178 --> 00:39:11.307
which nobody
knows is, um.. yeah...

00:39:11.349 --> 00:39:17.897
"See that sun in the morning,
peeking over the hill?

00:39:17.938 --> 00:39:24.695
I bet you know it always has,
I guess it always will.

00:39:24.737 --> 00:39:31.202
That's how I feel about someone.
Somebody feels about me.

00:39:31.202 --> 00:39:37.708
We know we love each other,
and that's the way it'll
always be."

00:39:37.708 --> 00:39:42.171
And then it goes on with the,
"Oh, we ain't got a barrel of
money...

00:39:42.213 --> 00:39:45.049
maybe we're ragged and funny.

00:39:45.049 --> 00:39:51.305
We'll travel along, singing a
song, side-by-side."

00:39:51.347 --> 00:39:57.228
You know, if I have my honey,
you know, we just stick together
and have spunk...

00:39:57.228 --> 00:39:59.021
we'll- we'll make it!

00:39:59.063 --> 00:40:02.900
And it actually comes out of the
music even more than I ever
realized.

00:40:02.942 --> 00:40:05.736
You know, I realize in my shows,
you know, half way in...

00:40:05.778 --> 00:40:11.534
I'll let everybody know, "You
know, this got everybody through
the Depression last time!"

00:40:13.577 --> 00:40:16.497
Where do ya live?
We moved.

00:40:16.539 --> 00:40:17.748
From where?

00:40:17.748 --> 00:40:20.418
Uh.. near my aunt's house.

00:40:20.459 --> 00:40:24.171
Well, you see, uh.. my aunt
lived a little bit away from
where we used to live.

00:40:24.213 --> 00:40:28.509
Uh.. she's a widow.
Her husband died.

00:40:28.551 --> 00:40:30.719
That's how she came
to be a widow.

00:40:30.761 --> 00:40:33.389
Uh.. the stock market
didn't kill him.

00:40:33.431 --> 00:40:35.724
No, he's one that the stock
market didn't kill.

00:40:35.766 --> 00:40:40.604
He died in September, BC, before
the crash. Very sick man.

00:40:40.604 --> 00:40:42.815
He had diabetes at 45.

00:40:42.857 --> 00:40:47.445
That's nothing,
I had Chrysler at 110.

00:40:47.486 --> 00:40:53.033
Eddie Canter was uh.. really one
of the people who really changed
our lives.

00:40:53.075 --> 00:40:56.120
Because he was on radio Sunday
nights at 8:00.

00:40:56.120 --> 00:41:02.209
And uh.. when he got on the air,
during the worst parts of uh..
the Depression...

00:41:02.251 --> 00:41:05.296
when people had no jobs, there
was no food on the table...

00:41:05.337 --> 00:41:09.800
he would sing like, "Potatoes
are cheaper, tomatoes are
cheaper...

00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:13.471
now's the time to fall in love."
He was encouraging people to
fall in love.

00:41:13.512 --> 00:41:16.515
Uh.. well that was nice...

00:41:16.557 --> 00:41:20.769
but he himself had gone through
a- a very tough time.

00:41:20.811 --> 00:41:24.315
He lost his home, he lost his
money, and uh..

00:41:24.315 --> 00:41:26.484
they were moving furniture out
of his house...

00:41:26.525 --> 00:41:30.988
and he was still able to go on
the radio and start cheering
people up.

00:41:30.988 --> 00:41:35.743
For those few minutes that we
saw those guys do their stuff...

00:41:35.784 --> 00:41:38.996
uh.. the whole world
was full of hope.

00:41:39.038 --> 00:41:42.541
Say, by-cracky, that reminds me
of something I forgot
to remember.

00:41:42.583 --> 00:41:46.420
Listen, next time you folks
have to give a present to a
bride and groom...

00:41:46.462 --> 00:41:52.426
be sure you make that present
an Aladdin kerosene mantle
lamp.

00:41:52.468 --> 00:41:55.513
Now, they can court in the dark
before they get married.

00:41:55.513 --> 00:42:00.476
You know, after a while, they
get fed up on it and need a
little light.

00:42:00.518 --> 00:42:03.729
Had a letter from a feller says
here he had a falling out with
his gal...

00:42:03.771 --> 00:42:08.651
and he- he wrote me he wanted
me to sing, "I only want a
buddy, not a sweetheart."

00:42:08.692 --> 00:42:13.906
I'll sing it for him, but I'll
bet a dollar he changes his
mind.

00:42:13.948 --> 00:42:20.746
"What is romance, but taking a
chance? Gambling with..."

00:42:20.788 --> 00:42:25.042
That's a good example of uh..
early broadcasts.

00:42:25.084 --> 00:42:28.546
Now these were called
syndicated shows.

00:42:28.587 --> 00:42:32.925
They're 16-inch transcriptions,
uh.. each 15 minutes per side.

00:42:32.967 --> 00:42:37.179
And uh.. these were pressed out
by uh...

00:42:37.221 --> 00:42:39.431
whoever put them out
for the sponsor.

00:42:39.473 --> 00:42:42.142
And they were sent out
to whatever radio stations...

00:42:42.184 --> 00:42:44.895
subscribed and- and- and wanted
to play these shows.

00:42:44.895 --> 00:42:48.649
And this quite obviously was
very uh..

00:42:48.691 --> 00:42:54.905
is aimed at a rural population,
and a population that would be
interested in buying uh..

00:42:54.905 --> 00:43:00.035
lamps, because they- they didn't
have electricity at that point.

00:43:00.077 --> 00:43:04.915
Uh.. this is a sample of a show
from the Coconut Grove
Orchestra...

00:43:04.957 --> 00:43:09.211
here in- in Los Angeles, which
was the preeminent nightclub...

00:43:09.253 --> 00:43:14.967
certainly on the West Coast, and
then one of the- the world's,
you know, prominent nightclubs.

00:43:15.009 --> 00:43:21.932
Uh.. so here's how the- here's
what the better half would be
listening to, so to speak.

00:43:21.932 --> 00:43:25.185
"This enchanting music has
thrilled your favorite star...

00:43:25.227 --> 00:43:28.772
and guided their dancing feet
in gay fantasy."

00:43:28.814 --> 00:43:31.984
( MUSIC PLAYING )

00:43:36.280 --> 00:43:42.244
While out in the farms, some
people had to de- depend on
lamps for their lighting...

00:43:42.286 --> 00:43:45.956
here in Los Angeles and other
metropolitan centers...

00:43:45.998 --> 00:43:49.043
even during the depths of the
depths of the Depression, uh..

00:43:49.084 --> 00:43:51.795
people were still having a good
time, you know?

00:43:51.837 --> 00:43:56.300
It was not as though the entire
population was destitute or
anything like that.

00:43:56.342 --> 00:44:00.262
You know, it- it- it certainly
affected some people more
than others.

00:44:00.304 --> 00:44:02.973
My uncle, by the way, was a
songwriter at that time.

00:44:02.973 --> 00:44:04.642
His name was Stanley Demerell.

00:44:04.683 --> 00:44:09.938
And he was writing in the depths
of the 1930s Depression in
England.

00:44:09.980 --> 00:44:12.608
And he- he wrote songs that were
complete sc- escapism.

00:44:12.650 --> 00:44:14.652
One of them is sung today.

00:44:14.652 --> 00:44:19.031
He wrote "Lady of Spain, I adore
you," and "Let's All Sing Like
the Birdies Sing."

00:44:19.073 --> 00:44:21.992
And he once explained why he
wrote these songs.

00:44:21.992 --> 00:44:24.078
He said, "Well," he said,
"my partner and I," he said...

00:44:24.119 --> 00:44:28.332
"we- we couldn't compete with
these American, slick American
love songs...

00:44:28.332 --> 00:44:31.460
that always seem to take place
on a couch indoors.

00:44:31.502 --> 00:44:35.964
So my partner and I decided we
needed to do songs that were
about the outside...

00:44:36.006 --> 00:44:38.175
like 'Lady of Spain.'"

00:44:38.217 --> 00:44:40.928
And my uncle told me that uh..
things were so bad that...

00:44:40.969 --> 00:44:45.849
some of his songwriting
colleagues, and you have to
listen very carefully to this...

00:44:45.849 --> 00:44:51.105
um.. some of his colleagues were
forced to pawn, p-a-w-n, their
own underwear.

00:44:51.146 --> 00:44:55.275
Which is something, you can't
get much lower than that.
Pawning your underwear.

00:45:04.827 --> 00:45:08.956
"Among the rugged peaks that
frown down upon the Borgo
Pass...

00:45:08.997 --> 00:45:13.627
are found crumbling castles
of a bygone age."

00:45:15.462 --> 00:45:20.342
"Among the rugged peaks that
frown down upon the Borgo
Pass...

00:45:20.384 --> 00:45:26.432
are found crumbling castles
of a bygone age."

00:45:28.726 --> 00:45:32.688
Hollywood was at that time a
little town...

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:36.233
a little country town.
You know?

00:45:36.275 --> 00:45:40.404
And it was wonderful.
I loved it!

00:45:40.404 --> 00:45:42.239
The movies kept going.

00:45:42.281 --> 00:45:47.995
And uh.. well, people needed
something else, you know...

00:45:48.036 --> 00:45:52.166
and to have their minds taken
off their tragedy...

00:45:52.207 --> 00:45:57.296
that goes on every day un- until
they get jobs.

00:46:01.550 --> 00:46:05.763
It was six cents for children,
and eleven cents for grownups.

00:46:05.804 --> 00:46:07.639
There was a penny tax.

00:46:07.681 --> 00:46:10.726
And I remember you standing in
front of the theaters...

00:46:10.768 --> 00:46:15.606
and when a- an adult would go
in with their 15 cents...

00:46:15.647 --> 00:46:17.608
they'd get four pennies back.

00:46:17.649 --> 00:46:21.195
And you'd say, "Can I have one
of your pennies, mister, to get
into the movie?"

00:46:21.236 --> 00:46:24.865
And they- most people
gave you the penny.

00:46:24.907 --> 00:46:27.951
But every now and then, some big
sport would give you four
cents...

00:46:27.951 --> 00:46:31.705
so you were well on your way
into the movie.

00:46:31.747 --> 00:46:33.957
Of course,
then once you got in...

00:46:33.999 --> 00:46:37.294
you opened the door
down at the bottom...

00:46:37.294 --> 00:46:39.463
and let two or three
of your friends in.

00:46:39.505 --> 00:46:43.717
And then there was a scramble
always, because when you opened
the door, the light...

00:46:43.759 --> 00:46:46.470
from the outside... because
they're usually during the of
course it was a matinee

00:46:46.470 --> 00:46:48.806
the light from outside would
come in...

00:46:48.806 --> 00:46:52.726
and the ushers would come
scrambling to find the kids
who'd just gotten in!

00:46:52.768 --> 00:46:55.145
There were the coming
attractions, which we still
have.

00:46:55.145 --> 00:46:58.315
That's all we have left of the
way people used to go to the
movies.

00:46:58.315 --> 00:47:00.984
Uh.. but when they-- back then
they would see short subjects...

00:47:01.026 --> 00:47:03.237
like a comedy short with Laurel
&amp; Hardy.

00:47:03.278 --> 00:47:08.075
Uh.. they might see a serial
chapter, you know, a western
that was continued next week.

00:47:08.116 --> 00:47:10.744
Uh.. they might see a newsreel.
They would see a cartoon.

00:47:10.786 --> 00:47:15.499
Uh.. there were travel logs. You
know, there were all sorts of-
of these subjects...

00:47:15.541 --> 00:47:19.002
and uh.. people were just used
to that, seeing a whole
variety...

00:47:19.044 --> 00:47:24.341
of different shorts
and then the big picture,
uh.. a full feature.

00:47:24.383 --> 00:47:29.596
Movies in the '30s ran the gamut
from extreme poverty row things.

00:47:29.638 --> 00:47:33.058
There were guys making westerns
for 900 bucks, you know.

00:47:33.100 --> 00:47:39.106
There were big pictures like
"Gone With The Wind" that were
made for multiple millions.

00:47:39.147 --> 00:47:43.026
Studios were making 40, 50,
60 pictures a year.

00:47:43.068 --> 00:47:47.739
A star could make two, or three
or four films a year, directors
the same.

00:47:47.781 --> 00:47:52.870
So today, a filmmaker might make
uh..a film every two or three
years.

00:47:52.911 --> 00:47:59.293
Whereas, John Ford in 1939 and
1940 made six, you know, major
classics that uh..

00:47:59.334 --> 00:48:04.172
are considered today. "Young Mr.
Lincoln" comes along, "The
Mohawk," "Grapes of Wrath,"

00:48:04.214 --> 00:48:11.221
uh.. "How Green Was My Valley?"
"Stagecoach," all within a very
brief span of time.

00:48:11.263 --> 00:48:13.557
And of course, the musicals,
too.

00:48:13.599 --> 00:48:17.311
You know, "The Gold Diggers
of 1932 then 1933."

00:48:17.352 --> 00:48:20.731
And all the girls came out, you
know, all had blonde hair...

00:48:20.731 --> 00:48:25.402
and all Busby Berkeley dance
routines. Very glamorous.

00:48:25.402 --> 00:48:32.367
And I grew up-- and we all did--
we all grew up with this fantasy
world.

00:48:34.953 --> 00:48:38.457
Movies were important.
I saw "Things to Come."

00:48:38.498 --> 00:48:43.045
It's a film by H.G. Wells.
It came out in 1936.

00:48:43.086 --> 00:48:47.883
And when I saw that film,
I staggered out of that film,
and I said...

00:48:47.925 --> 00:48:51.678
"The future's going to belong to
me! I'm going to make the
future."

00:48:51.720 --> 00:48:56.058
Oh God, is there never to be any
age of happiness? Is there never
to be any rest?

00:48:56.099 --> 00:49:01.897
Rest enough for the individual
man, too much and too soon,
and we call it Death.

00:49:01.939 --> 00:49:08.612
But for Man, no rest and no
ending. He must go on, conquest
beyond conquest.

00:49:08.654 --> 00:49:15.452
Poor humanity is so fragile, so
weak. Little- little animals.

00:49:15.452 --> 00:49:17.829
Little animals.

00:49:17.871 --> 00:49:22.334
If we're no more than animals,
we must snatch each little scrap
of happiness...

00:49:22.376 --> 00:49:26.964
and live and suffer and pass.

00:49:26.964 --> 00:49:30.842
It is this or that.

00:49:30.884 --> 00:49:35.889
All the universe or nothingness.

00:49:35.931 --> 00:49:40.143
Which shall it be, Passworthy?

00:49:40.185 --> 00:49:42.229
Which shall it be?

00:49:42.270 --> 00:49:46.316
Well, one of the things I think
that has been misstated about
films of the '30s...

00:49:46.316 --> 00:49:50.237
in the past is that um.. the
movies provided escapism.

00:49:50.278 --> 00:49:55.075
And it's true, there were a lot
of things like Fred Astaire and-
and musicals and comedies...

00:49:55.117 --> 00:50:00.080
W.C. Fields and Bob Hope and the
Marx Brothers provided escapist
entertainment.

00:50:00.122 --> 00:50:01.999
What's the show about?

00:50:01.999 --> 00:50:03.291
It's all about the Depression.

00:50:03.333 --> 00:50:05.127
We won't have to rehearse that.

00:50:05.168 --> 00:50:08.588
But there were just as many
films perhaps that--

00:50:08.630 --> 00:50:11.174
that dealt with the reality
of the Depression.

00:50:11.216 --> 00:50:16.179
But they have not been the ones
that have been revived because
they're--

00:50:16.179 --> 00:50:19.016
some of them are pretty grim.

00:50:23.437 --> 00:50:27.024
The "Wizard of Oz," the book and
especially the movie...

00:50:27.065 --> 00:50:33.864
were also about monetary policy,
about deflation, there not being
enough money.

00:50:33.864 --> 00:50:39.369
So the word Oz is short for
ounce, there's the yellow brick
road...

00:50:39.411 --> 00:50:43.623
the gold question which was so
salient in that period.

00:50:43.665 --> 00:50:46.626
The very dark part in the
beginning, the black and white
part.

00:50:46.668 --> 00:50:50.380
That's how people felt about
monetary policy, they knew there
wasn't enough money...

00:50:50.380 --> 00:50:53.175
but they also knew, uh.. they
viewed it as like the weather.

00:50:53.216 --> 00:50:56.803
There was nothing they could do
about it, it was like a storm
coming.

00:50:56.845 --> 00:50:59.097
And they just had to weather it.

00:50:59.139 --> 00:51:02.517
The character Dorothy,
she finds her own way.

00:51:02.559 --> 00:51:07.105
This disastrous situation,
and she manages sort of, um..

00:51:07.147 --> 00:51:11.777
by luck to slay at least one
witch and then another by- by
cleverness.

00:51:11.818 --> 00:51:15.280
And she actually manages to uh..
tame the Wizard.

00:51:15.322 --> 00:51:20.535
So tame the money.
And that was kind of a fantasy.

00:51:20.577 --> 00:51:25.123
verybody points to the '30s
as great golden age...

00:51:25.165 --> 00:51:28.418
of the filmmaking process.

00:51:28.418 --> 00:51:33.256
But remember that the great
filmmakers of the '30s...

00:51:33.256 --> 00:51:37.928
they had learned their craft
in silent films.

00:51:37.969 --> 00:51:42.265
The craft
of visual storytelling.

00:51:42.307 --> 00:51:45.435
Hitchcock...y mentor, Alfred

00:51:45.435 --> 00:51:49.356
saying that with the
advent of sound...

00:51:49.397 --> 00:51:54.027
we lost the art form
of the moving image.

00:51:54.069 --> 00:51:57.155
When talkies came in, there was
a greater sense of realism.

00:51:57.197 --> 00:52:02.327
Silent films had been almost
dreamlike in the way they
presented their stories.

00:52:02.369 --> 00:52:04.454
And- and sound brought
a realism to the movies.

00:52:04.496 --> 00:52:09.709
And uh.. you saw that manifested
in things like gangster pictures
and backstage dramas.

00:52:09.751 --> 00:52:12.129
And you saw more stories about
working-class people...

00:52:12.129 --> 00:52:16.299
as well as fantasy-escape things
with- with uh.. rich folks in-
in jeopardy.

00:52:16.299 --> 00:52:20.137
How would you feel,
that looks real.

00:52:20.137 --> 00:52:23.140
Ah, just a joke,
don't mind them.

00:52:23.140 --> 00:52:28.979
Roll all your troubles into a
cigarette...

00:52:29.020 --> 00:52:33.441
and puff your blues away.

00:52:46.037 --> 00:52:50.584
People expected a cartoon
with their movie show.

00:52:50.625 --> 00:52:52.502
You could have-- you could leave
out the newsreel.

00:52:52.502 --> 00:52:55.881
You could leave out two-reel
comedy. But you had to have a
cartoon.

00:52:55.922 --> 00:53:01.052
In fact, there's a famous 1930s
song that's called, "What? No
Mickey Mouse?

00:53:01.094 --> 00:53:02.637
What kind of program is this?"

00:53:02.679 --> 00:53:04.598
And that- that was a song that
was popular.

00:53:04.639 --> 00:53:07.350
Because it was it was theatre-
theatre didn't show a cartoon.

00:53:07.350 --> 00:53:08.894
That's what it was about.

00:53:11.146 --> 00:53:14.983
Disney was moving animation from
this, what they call rubber hose
crazy style...

00:53:15.025 --> 00:53:18.528
of a- of drawing to a more
realistic style of storytelling.

00:53:18.570 --> 00:53:24.534
And all the studios followed
Disney. But in the early '30s,
the depths of the Depression...

00:53:24.534 --> 00:53:28.538
those cartoons are particularly
like psychedelic.

00:53:28.538 --> 00:53:31.708
They're- they're mind blowing.

00:53:31.750 --> 00:53:38.673
Mimi, you funny little good for
nothing Mimi.

00:53:38.715 --> 00:53:42.219
Am I the guy?

00:53:42.260 --> 00:53:44.888
Mimi, you sunny little honey
of a Mimi.

00:53:44.930 --> 00:53:47.724
There was no- no realism here.
And the stories themselves are
sort of--

00:53:47.766 --> 00:53:49.976
these guys were just making it
up as they went along.

00:53:50.018 --> 00:53:54.064
Storytelling even as in a short
cartoon wasn't sophisticated...

00:53:54.064 --> 00:53:55.857
as it would be 10 years later...

00:53:55.899 --> 00:53:58.526
when they made Bugs Bunny
cartoons and Daffy Duck
cartoons.

00:53:58.568 --> 00:54:02.030
In this early '30s period, uh..
they might start the story in
one place...

00:54:02.072 --> 00:54:04.908
and go outside and then suddenly
they're going somewhere else.

00:54:04.950 --> 00:54:07.244
They would just come up with
these stores, and they would
just, uh..

00:54:07.244 --> 00:54:11.414
kinda draw them, you know, uh..
free form, you know, stream
of consciousness.

00:54:11.414 --> 00:54:13.208
That's how they look.

00:54:15.418 --> 00:54:22.342
My first paycheck is from--
really, paycheck is from uh...
Walt Disney Studio.

00:54:22.384 --> 00:54:26.513
I was on "Bambi"
for three and a half year.

00:54:26.554 --> 00:54:29.057
So that was- that was ideal.

00:54:29.099 --> 00:54:33.561
They didn't pay me very much,
but then I loved the job.

00:54:41.945 --> 00:54:43.613
Going back to the WPA...

00:54:43.613 --> 00:54:46.032
I remember S. Macdonald,
Stanton Macdonald-Wright.

00:54:46.074 --> 00:54:47.575
He was the director.

00:54:47.617 --> 00:54:50.161
I brought my painting in there
and showed it to him.

00:54:50.203 --> 00:54:53.456
So we got paid $96 per month...

00:54:53.456 --> 00:54:55.709
So you turn- turn in two oil
paintings...

00:54:55.750 --> 00:54:59.337
and two watercolor
or else four lithographs.

00:54:59.379 --> 00:55:02.007
So that's like, getting through
the WPA, that's a good thing.

00:55:02.048 --> 00:55:06.303
That saved a lot of artists
from starving to death.

00:55:06.303 --> 00:55:10.015
Narrator: The sensitive fingers
of artists are poorly suited to
manual labor.

00:55:10.056 --> 00:55:13.018
And in finding suitable work
for musicians and other
artists...

00:55:13.059 --> 00:55:16.771
the WPA has contributed greatly
to the culture of America.

00:55:16.813 --> 00:55:19.983
Painters, too, contribute their
bit to making the works
program...

00:55:19.983 --> 00:55:22.152
a real and permanent
accomplishment.

00:55:22.152 --> 00:55:24.362
These reproductions of the
American scene of today...

00:55:24.404 --> 00:55:28.199
will make this one of the most
fertile periods of our
country's art.

00:55:28.241 --> 00:55:33.288
( MUSIC PLAYING )

00:55:33.330 --> 00:55:35.081
Some of this work is done on
canvas...

00:55:35.123 --> 00:55:37.876
but much of it is created on the
walls of our schools...

00:55:37.917 --> 00:55:42.005
libraries and other public
buildings in the form of mural
paintings.

00:55:43.631 --> 00:55:46.593
The fact that the government
would experiment...

00:55:46.634 --> 00:55:50.680
with the idea of putting
creative people to work and...

00:55:50.680 --> 00:55:53.850
and getting a public good out
of it uh...

00:55:53.850 --> 00:55:58.021
was really piloted here
at Coit Tower in San Francisco.

00:55:58.021 --> 00:56:03.068
That really was the pilot
program that had helped to
launch the WPA...

00:56:03.109 --> 00:56:07.030
the Works Progress
Administration's use of artists.

00:56:07.072 --> 00:56:11.076
So the 26 artists who got
involved and were
commissioned...

00:56:11.117 --> 00:56:16.581
to create these murals here at
Coit Tower were really uh.. the
pioneers.

00:56:16.623 --> 00:56:19.167
The sort of-- the experiment
that worked.

00:56:19.209 --> 00:56:24.339
And then it allowed public art
to spread across the whole
country.

00:56:24.381 --> 00:56:28.385
It's a school of art called
social realism, and uh...

00:56:28.426 --> 00:56:31.721
that's most of what's depicted
in these murals.

00:56:31.721 --> 00:56:35.100
And social realism was really
about capturing everyday life...

00:56:35.141 --> 00:56:40.563
and helping to tell the story
of the average person, um...

00:56:40.563 --> 00:56:43.566
as opposed to just what,
you know the--

00:56:43.608 --> 00:56:48.530
the news and the media sort of-
would sort of highlight, back
then, and even today...

00:56:48.571 --> 00:56:50.782
which is the story of the great
man, right?

00:56:50.824 --> 00:56:52.617
The- the captains of industry.

00:56:52.659 --> 00:56:57.455
And there is a- a- an
opportunity of creating
community...

00:56:57.497 --> 00:56:59.457
when you deal with a lot
of public art.

00:56:59.499 --> 00:57:03.545
And that's certainly been the
philosophy of many of the
murals...

00:57:03.586 --> 00:57:09.426
that we have been commissioned
by the Arts Commission over the
years here in San Francisco.

00:57:12.679 --> 00:57:17.809
We are at Lucy Flower High
School, and this is the foyer
of the school...

00:57:17.851 --> 00:57:24.607
and this mural was created
between 1938 and 1940 by Edward
Millman.

00:57:24.607 --> 00:57:27.444
He was an artist
that was on the WPA.

00:57:27.444 --> 00:57:32.574
And he wanted to create a mural
about outstanding American
women.

00:57:32.615 --> 00:57:37.454
And at the same time, he was
studying a lot about the Mexican
muralist movement.

00:57:37.454 --> 00:57:41.458
And prior to creating this, he
had actually gone to Mexico...

00:57:41.458 --> 00:57:45.086
and studied with Diego Rivera
in 1934.

00:57:45.128 --> 00:57:49.632
So when he was sort of building
the agenda for this image...

00:57:49.632 --> 00:57:53.511
he wanted to really promote that
kind of social realism...

00:57:53.553 --> 00:57:57.807
within this sort of temple
that you see here.

00:57:57.849 --> 00:58:01.019
It took him two years to
complete uh.. this image.

00:58:01.060 --> 00:58:05.732
And then one year later, it was
deemed by the Board of
Education...

00:58:05.773 --> 00:58:08.902
as misery-laden and depressing.

00:58:08.943 --> 00:58:10.695
So they whitewashed
it one year later.

00:58:10.737 --> 00:58:15.992
This image is probably the most
controversial in the city
of Chicago...

00:58:16.034 --> 00:58:18.828
because it was so heavy.

00:58:18.870 --> 00:58:22.749
And it was talking about so many
core truths...

00:58:22.790 --> 00:58:25.752
to what was happening
at the time.

00:58:25.793 --> 00:58:28.796
thing to remember is...rtant

00:58:28.838 --> 00:58:32.675
artists are like a barometer
of what's happening.

00:58:32.717 --> 00:58:38.640
They record what's going on,
they tell these stories in
creative and imaginative ways.

00:58:38.681 --> 00:58:45.313
And when that kind of
philosophical and intellectual
creativity is covered up...

00:58:45.355 --> 00:58:49.400
that's- that's something that
is really hard to take.

00:58:49.442 --> 00:58:54.489
So when you walked into this
room, it was completely white
in 1996.

00:58:54.531 --> 00:59:01.329
And so we did test after test to
try to figure out how to unveil
this powerful image.

00:59:01.371 --> 00:59:05.333
It was a pretty magical time
for us. That was in 1997.

00:59:05.375 --> 00:59:09.963
And it took us about nine months
to move throughout this entire
room...

00:59:10.004 --> 00:59:12.590
and then we had an unveiling
ceremony.

00:59:12.632 --> 00:59:17.428
And it was all about unveiling
the history that it
encapsulated.

00:59:17.470 --> 00:59:24.227
So yes, it was very precious
to all of us involved.
And remains so.

00:59:24.269 --> 00:59:29.274
And one of the main themes that
emerges in- in- in the arts of
the '30s...

00:59:29.315 --> 00:59:33.486
and the whole culture of the
'30s, is- is the one that
Hemingway...

00:59:33.528 --> 00:59:40.076
eventually comes to that the
individual alone doesn't
have a chance.

00:59:40.118 --> 00:59:45.248
And so this idea that people
need other people...

00:59:45.248 --> 00:59:49.460
that the individual can be
broken, but then maybe stronger.

00:59:49.502 --> 00:59:55.133
But- but- but so much of the-
of- of the art of the period was
moving toward a sense...

00:59:55.174 --> 00:59:57.302
and- and the politics
of the period...

00:59:57.343 --> 01:00:02.890
moving toward a sense
of necessity politically
for empathy, for care...

01:00:02.932 --> 01:00:06.936
for, you know, brotherhood,
for collective action...

01:00:06.936 --> 01:00:12.817
whether it's in strikes or in,
you know, the implementation
of a Social Security system.

01:00:12.859 --> 01:00:18.615
At the same time, it's also
strongly resisted by- by- by
uh...

01:00:18.615 --> 01:00:21.534
a whole other, you know,
political set.

01:00:21.576 --> 01:00:26.164
So- so the '30s becomes a really
polarized period politically...

01:00:26.205 --> 01:00:31.252
you know, between uh.. you know,
between these collectivist
notions...

01:00:31.294 --> 01:00:36.299
and the notions of- of the
individual that are still fairly
strong um...

01:00:36.341 --> 01:00:38.259
in- in this country.

01:00:38.301 --> 01:00:41.262
( MUSIC PLAYING )

01:00:42.639 --> 01:00:44.974
( APPLAUSE )

01:00:48.770 --> 01:00:52.190
You and you and you and you
you've got a President now.

01:00:52.231 --> 01:00:56.277
He gave the land a new deal! You
hold the cards, now you deal.

01:00:56.319 --> 01:00:59.656
You and you and you and you put
shoulders to the plow.

01:00:59.656 --> 01:01:03.368
He gave us what we asked for,
now pay him back somehow!

01:01:03.409 --> 01:01:07.622
Step out in front,
get back of the President.

01:01:07.664 --> 01:01:10.667
And give a man a job.

01:01:10.708 --> 01:01:13.878
Franklin Roosevelt's achievemenT
is all the more remarkable...

01:01:13.920 --> 01:01:17.548
because he had not only to
invent the tools with which to
fight the Depression...

01:01:17.590 --> 01:01:20.677
but he had to convince people
that it was legitimate to do
anything at all.

01:01:20.677 --> 01:01:23.137
And there were a lot of people
who were very, very skeptical...

01:01:23.179 --> 01:01:28.017
that there was anything that
should be done here other than
just let the thing go down...

01:01:28.017 --> 01:01:31.020
the drain, and eventually it
would take care of itself.

01:01:44.659 --> 01:01:49.163
There was a woman named Lorena
Hickok that was employed by the
Roosevelt administration...

01:01:49.205 --> 01:01:52.041
to go around the country and
write reports about how
people...

01:01:52.041 --> 01:01:54.627
were dealing with the economic
difficulties.

01:01:54.669 --> 01:01:57.338
And, you know, what- what she
really came to say in--

01:01:57.380 --> 01:02:00.591
and especially like in the Farm
Belt and- and different parts of
the country...

01:02:00.633 --> 01:02:03.928
is that she saw this kind of
prevailing sense of despair.

01:02:03.970 --> 01:02:06.556
You know, people were becoming
apathetic, you know?

01:02:06.556 --> 01:02:09.559
And- and I- I think to a certain
extent, you know, "The New
Deal"...

01:02:09.600 --> 01:02:12.019
did play a role in kind of
letting people know, you know...

01:02:12.061 --> 01:02:16.023
we're not going to let things
continue in- continue on as
they- as they have been.

01:02:16.065 --> 01:02:18.234
You know, we are going to try
and improve the situation.

01:02:18.276 --> 01:02:20.862
And I think that meant a lot
to a lot of people.

01:02:20.903 --> 01:02:23.114
Well, I remember sitting
by the radio...

01:02:23.156 --> 01:02:29.162
whole family sitting by the
radio listening to a fireside
chat, you know, Roosevelt.

01:02:29.203 --> 01:02:31.748
PRESIDENT ROOSEVELT:
To assert my firm belief...

01:02:31.748 --> 01:02:38.254
that the only thing we have
to fear is fear itself!

01:02:40.757 --> 01:02:43.050
I want my money!
Let go of the bank!

01:02:43.092 --> 01:02:45.428
Let me get my money!

01:02:49.432 --> 01:02:51.184
Doctor! Doctor!

01:02:58.775 --> 01:03:00.735
Doctor!

01:03:09.368 --> 01:03:11.537
Doctor! Doctor!

01:03:11.579 --> 01:03:13.956
Doctor! Doctor!

01:03:14.957 --> 01:03:16.876
There's your Doctor.

01:03:16.918 --> 01:03:19.962
( HAIL TO THE CHIEF PLAYING )

01:03:28.137 --> 01:03:32.058
Mr. President, what will
cure a Depression?"

01:03:32.099 --> 01:03:37.313
Confidence can lick
this old Depression!

01:03:37.313 --> 01:03:41.317
Confidence, hey, hey!
It's our salvation!

01:03:41.359 --> 01:03:44.362
Preach it, tout it,
shout it!

01:03:44.403 --> 01:03:48.449
Confidence!
Just have confidence!

01:03:56.374 --> 01:04:00.545
Wake up, wake up
Open up the door

01:04:00.586 --> 01:04:04.382
Hey there I know
We'll come back with more

01:04:04.423 --> 01:04:11.097
Yesterday there were skies of
Gray but today is another day

01:04:12.473 --> 01:04:16.018
Go worm go worm
We'll all get a break

01:04:16.018 --> 01:04:20.439
New day's dawning
For worker and dawn

01:04:20.481 --> 01:04:23.985
No more bumming
We'll all get to work

01:04:24.026 --> 01:04:29.699
There's a new day dawning now

01:04:29.699 --> 01:04:32.159
I'll think we'll win out.

01:04:32.201 --> 01:04:38.499
Not because of a government,
not even because of our leaders.

01:04:38.541 --> 01:04:42.879
But because as a people,
we've had a vision...

01:04:42.879 --> 01:04:48.551
and we've worked for it
and we've seen it through.
Thank you.

01:04:48.551 --> 01:04:50.970
The President of the United
States, inspired by the way...

01:04:51.012 --> 01:04:57.643
by his wife, Eleanor, uh..
at least, no- I'd say
inspired...

01:04:57.685 --> 01:05:01.022
directed by Eleanor...

01:05:01.063 --> 01:05:05.026
tended to want to give
to the community...

01:05:05.067 --> 01:05:07.528
greater opportunities
then they had enjoyed.

01:05:07.570 --> 01:05:14.577
I pledge myself to a New Deal
for the American people!

01:05:14.619 --> 01:05:17.580
It opened thoughts...

01:05:17.622 --> 01:05:21.918
opportunities about
out of the New Deal...

01:05:21.959 --> 01:05:25.880
can we begin to create
a real deal?

01:05:25.922 --> 01:05:28.424
Some people felt that FDR was
the closest thing to keeping us
from getting--

01:05:28.424 --> 01:05:30.635
having a revolution
in this country.

01:05:30.676 --> 01:05:33.930
In other words, he was providing
work with the WPA and all these
different projects...

01:05:33.930 --> 01:05:37.433
that if that wouldn't have
happened there are those who
contended...

01:05:37.433 --> 01:05:38.935
we would have had a bloody
revolution.

01:05:38.935 --> 01:05:40.937
So you had a lot of social
unrest at that time.

01:05:40.937 --> 01:05:44.941
Not like it was necessarily over
in Europe, but you had people
that were out of work.

01:05:44.941 --> 01:05:48.819
They were hungry. They- they
looked at fascism or
communism...

01:05:48.861 --> 01:05:52.114
and that gave them- that gave
them a chance, an opportunity
maybe to climb up the ladder.

01:05:52.114 --> 01:05:54.742
Maybe there's something wrong
with our system. Maybe we ought
to do that.

01:05:54.784 --> 01:05:57.244
So you- you saw that even at
that time as a youngster.

01:05:57.286 --> 01:06:01.123
I remember arguments between my-
my father and other people about
political things like that.

01:06:01.165 --> 01:06:03.542
Dealing not so much about
Washington politics...

01:06:03.584 --> 01:06:06.796
but what the situation would
have to be-- we'd have to go the
European route.

01:06:06.796 --> 01:06:11.467
In other words, either fascism
or communism, because that's the
way to do things right.

01:06:11.467 --> 01:06:14.679
The- the New Deal, for all
of its historical consequence...

01:06:14.720 --> 01:06:17.264
it failed to end
the Great Depression.

01:06:17.306 --> 01:06:21.852
But it did something else, and
it did something I think that
was part of a very high level...

01:06:21.894 --> 01:06:25.856
visionary, political calculus
that Roosevelt had in mind.

01:06:25.898 --> 01:06:32.613
It used the moment of this
enduring crisis to put in place
a set of major reforms...

01:06:32.655 --> 01:06:36.033
of institutional rearrangement
of the whole social and economic
landscape...

01:06:36.075 --> 01:06:39.829
that stabilized the economy once
it recovered with World War
II...

01:06:39.870 --> 01:06:45.084
and provided the latticework
or the scaffolding on which
the American economy...

01:06:45.126 --> 01:06:47.461
and indeed the international
economy, uh..

01:06:47.503 --> 01:06:51.173
grew like crazy for a half
century after World War II.

01:06:51.173 --> 01:06:52.758
You're always going
to have boom and bust.

01:06:52.800 --> 01:06:55.344
You don't want-- and you even
have panics to some degree...

01:06:55.344 --> 01:06:58.222
but you- you don't want those
to become big Depressions...

01:06:58.264 --> 01:07:02.184
meaning output goes down quite
a lot and then stays down,
and you struggle to recover.

01:07:02.226 --> 01:07:03.644
You lose a decade.
You can lose a decade!

01:07:03.686 --> 01:07:06.272
And we know from the Japanese
experience recently...

01:07:06.313 --> 01:07:08.607
it can happen even today in
relatively rich countries.

01:07:08.649 --> 01:07:13.070
So I-I don't think there should
be any presumption that we've
grown out of depressions.

01:07:13.112 --> 01:07:16.782
Right? Maybe we've learned some
things about how to avoid them;
maybe we haven't.

01:07:16.824 --> 01:07:19.702
But I think that- that's what
the discussion should really
focus on.

01:07:22.872 --> 01:07:25.708
It was crisis, it was meltdown.

01:07:31.047 --> 01:07:37.845
We feared that all the financial
sector, uh... banks and security
firms may go down.

01:07:37.887 --> 01:07:40.723
The Lost Decade is a period...

01:07:40.723 --> 01:07:44.810
of e- extremely low economic
growth for the Japanese economy.

01:07:44.852 --> 01:07:49.523
So the business were not uh..
doing very well in general.

01:07:49.565 --> 01:07:55.112
The sort of norm was that after
you graduated from the
college...

01:07:55.154 --> 01:08:00.576
and you uh... you go
for a large firm.

01:08:00.576 --> 01:08:04.580
And a large firm
will keep you for life.

01:08:04.622 --> 01:08:11.212
So you are on the track, and you
can plan your- your life at the
age of 22.

01:08:11.253 --> 01:08:14.715
That was the sort
of typical picture.

01:08:14.757 --> 01:08:18.928
Because of the lifetime
employment system, they cannot
fire the core workers.

01:08:18.969 --> 01:08:25.184
So what the many Japanese
companies did during the Lost
Decade of the 1990's...

01:08:25.226 --> 01:08:27.853
is to keep the core employees...

01:08:27.895 --> 01:08:31.774
to keep the workers on the
lifetime employment system...

01:08:31.774 --> 01:08:34.110
but stopped hiring.

01:08:34.110 --> 01:08:37.071
So the first hit was not the
company employees...

01:08:37.113 --> 01:08:41.826
but the fresh out of college.

01:08:41.867 --> 01:08:43.119
They couldn't find, you know,
the- this- this famous uh...

01:08:43.119 --> 01:08:45.496
lifetime uh... employment.

01:08:45.538 --> 01:08:49.959
We made a lost generation.

01:08:49.959 --> 01:08:53.254
Belonging to some community
organization...

01:08:53.295 --> 01:08:55.673
it might be family
or company or school...

01:08:55.714 --> 01:09:00.261
whatever, is very important.
Very important.

01:09:00.302 --> 01:09:06.142
And uh.. actually company,
corporation, did a very
important thing.

01:09:06.183 --> 01:09:10.062
Japanese companies,
companies are heavily
in charge of the uh..

01:09:10.104 --> 01:09:13.816
sustaining many social security
or social importance,
practically.

01:09:13.816 --> 01:09:15.526
Rather than government itself.

01:09:15.568 --> 01:09:20.447
But uh.. you know, the lost
generation didn't have a chance
to--

01:09:20.489 --> 01:09:23.617
to belong to that kind of,
you know, community.

01:10:24.220 --> 01:10:27.973
Stability of the society,
or stable healthy growth...

01:10:28.015 --> 01:10:33.562
or development of the society,
we have to rebuild that kind
of community.

01:10:33.604 --> 01:10:36.065
So that's really our
challenge...

01:10:36.106 --> 01:10:41.695
that the answer is not getting
back- simply getting back to any
old system.

01:10:41.737 --> 01:10:44.907
We have to rebuild the concept
of the corporation.

01:10:44.907 --> 01:10:49.370
We- we have to rebuild the
concept of the employment or
labor.

01:10:49.411 --> 01:10:51.038
So that's really a real
challenge.

01:10:51.080 --> 01:10:55.751
So we really need just sort of
distractive, I mean, creation.

01:11:07.596 --> 01:11:11.558
What we can clearly see now is
to avoid what happened in Japan,

01:11:11.600 --> 01:11:13.852
to avoid a lost decade here in
the United States...

01:11:13.894 --> 01:11:16.689
which at this point may or may
not be unavoidable...

01:11:16.730 --> 01:11:20.901
um, really, honestly, is that we
need a massive injection...

01:11:20.943 --> 01:11:23.946
a continued massive injection
of economic stimulus.

01:11:23.988 --> 01:11:26.240
The question is, "Where is this
going to come from?"

01:11:26.282 --> 01:11:30.828
The economy has four basic areas
that we have um.. that drive
economic growth.

01:11:30.869 --> 01:11:34.707
In the United States, about 70
percent of economic growth comes
from consumers.

01:11:34.748 --> 01:11:39.962
Well, consumers have declining
home values, declining stock
portfolios...

01:11:40.004 --> 01:11:42.548
one in ten of them nearly
is out of a job.

01:11:42.589 --> 01:11:45.759
So that's really not going to be
a place where we're going to see
a lot of demand growing...

01:11:45.801 --> 01:11:48.012
in the- in the short
to medium term.

01:11:48.053 --> 01:11:52.141
Until the- until jobs come back,
consumption is not going to
drive our economy forward...

01:11:52.141 --> 01:11:53.892
it's not where we're going to
see our recovery.

01:11:53.934 --> 01:11:56.270
And that's 70 percent
of our economy...

01:11:56.312 --> 01:11:59.148
so we've got to make up for that
big chunk someplace else.

01:11:59.189 --> 01:12:02.860
The other three places we can
look to are business
investment...

01:12:02.901 --> 01:12:05.779
export growth,
and government spending.

01:12:05.821 --> 01:12:08.157
Businesses aren't going to
invest until they see consumers.

01:12:08.198 --> 01:12:09.783
So there's a little bit
of chicken and egg problem
going on there.

01:12:09.825 --> 01:12:11.910
Exports are up a little bit...

01:12:11.952 --> 01:12:14.455
but still it's a relatively
small share of our overall
economy...

01:12:14.496 --> 01:12:19.001
so it's not clear how that- that
relatively small share can pull
the whole US economy out.

01:12:19.043 --> 01:12:21.670
And then the last place we can
look is in terms of government
spending...

01:12:21.670 --> 01:12:23.797
to create economic demand.

01:12:23.839 --> 01:12:26.175
That's exactly what we did
during the Great Depression.

01:12:26.175 --> 01:12:29.011
And it's something that we
haven't really needed in uhm..

01:12:29.053 --> 01:12:33.557
in the same way that we need it
now or any point since the Great
Depression.

01:12:35.726 --> 01:12:39.521
( INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC PLAYING )

01:12:41.857 --> 01:12:46.820
I think we progress in lurches.
I think we lurch forward.

01:12:46.862 --> 01:12:50.866
I think we adopt a set of rules
and a vocabulary...

01:12:50.866 --> 01:12:55.954
and a standard for truth, and
that serves us well, this is
certainly true in science.

01:12:55.996 --> 01:12:59.500
It serves us well right up until
the moment where all of a
sudden...

01:12:59.541 --> 01:13:03.504
it isn't working,
and the whole world comes apart.

01:13:03.545 --> 01:13:05.547
We don't know how to change it
gradually.

01:13:05.589 --> 01:13:08.133
We just have to wait
for the iceberg.

01:13:08.175 --> 01:13:13.806
And then a wonderful thing
happens. The world falls apart.

01:13:13.847 --> 01:13:17.893
And we are able to stand there
and say...

01:13:17.935 --> 01:13:23.482
"What are we really trying to do
here?" What is our world really
like?

01:13:23.524 --> 01:13:26.860
And one of our most important
resources in this country
is our creativity.

01:13:26.902 --> 01:13:31.115
You know, and- and that- and
that if we can really kind of
deploy that, you know...

01:13:31.156 --> 01:13:34.451
I- I think in many respects the
answers to kind of working
ourselves out...

01:13:34.493 --> 01:13:38.288
of these economic troubles may
not come from that, you know,
the Congress or you know...

01:13:38.330 --> 01:13:40.999
or you know, the House of
Representatives or, you know...

01:13:41.041 --> 01:13:43.419
big corporations
or the Federal Reserve.

01:13:43.460 --> 01:13:46.755
But it may be something, you
know, things that go on in
people's garages, you know?

01:13:46.797 --> 01:13:50.384
Things like sustainable living
tips, you know, alternative
currencies.

01:13:50.426 --> 01:13:52.594
We're going to see a lot
of things happening on the
ground...

01:13:52.636 --> 01:13:54.763
that are going to be kind
of important innovations.

01:13:54.763 --> 01:13:57.724
We're- we're-- I- I think it's
kind of an inborn American
trait...

01:13:57.766 --> 01:14:00.310
that we have a tendency to kind
of tinker and innovate...

01:14:00.352 --> 01:14:03.897
when we're not happy with
existing or outdated systems.

01:14:06.900 --> 01:14:11.947
If you look historically,
a lot of strong businesses...

01:14:11.989 --> 01:14:14.324
were started during a- a
downturn in the economy...

01:14:14.366 --> 01:14:18.162
during the Great Depression,
or other recessions we've had.

01:14:18.203 --> 01:14:20.789
But, I mean, I was an investment
banker working 90 hours a
week...

01:14:20.831 --> 01:14:23.292
I'd- I'd never have the time to
launch something like that.

01:14:23.292 --> 01:14:27.129
Well, once I got laid off,
schedule opened up.

01:14:27.129 --> 01:14:30.132
And I thought, "Well, now is as
good a time as any."

01:14:30.132 --> 01:14:32.968
All jobs seem to be so hard
to get now...

01:14:33.010 --> 01:14:37.806
that I think you're seeing
people really just go after
where their passions are...

01:14:37.806 --> 01:14:41.143
as opposed to, "Well, this is
where the money is."

01:14:41.143 --> 01:14:44.188
So I think you're seeing a
number of paradigm shifts...

01:14:44.229 --> 01:14:47.816
more so than before in value--
shift in values, I think.

01:14:47.858 --> 01:14:52.654
And- and I-- my hope is that
they'll last, uhm.. so that
we'll have--

01:14:52.696 --> 01:14:57.284
that there'll be more of a gap
between these- these boom and
bust periods.

01:14:57.326 --> 01:14:59.953
And so we'll see. People tend to
have short memories, I think...

01:14:59.995 --> 01:15:04.041
particularly when times
do start to turn around.

01:15:05.834 --> 01:15:08.128
Welcome to New York City,
financial capital of the world.

01:15:08.170 --> 01:15:11.089
Welcome to Wall Street, the
heart of American capitalism.

01:15:11.131 --> 01:15:13.842
I was a former Vice President
at Deutsche Bank.

01:15:13.842 --> 01:15:17.054
There I helped manage a billion
dollar trading business.

01:15:17.095 --> 01:15:21.141
The crisis displaces a lot of
people. You know, I lost my job.

01:15:21.183 --> 01:15:24.269
You know, It's Adam Smith,
invisible hand...

01:15:24.311 --> 01:15:28.565
smacking me around and putting
me into a different area of the
economy.

01:15:28.607 --> 01:15:32.194
But uh.. for me, you know, I'm-
I'm really happy with what I'm
doing now.

01:15:32.236 --> 01:15:36.114
Showing people Wall Street.
Telling my story.
I love to teach.

01:15:36.156 --> 01:15:38.534
Some people uh.. find
inspiration.

01:15:38.534 --> 01:15:42.329
You know, it's-- they're saying,
"Hey, here's a guy making
lemonade out of lemons."

01:15:42.371 --> 01:15:44.456
You know, other people say...

01:15:44.498 --> 01:15:48.544
"You know, hey, here's a trader
that's been downgraded to a tour
guide," you know?

01:15:48.585 --> 01:15:50.754
It's two different ways
to look at it.

01:15:50.796 --> 01:15:57.636
Two years ago I was fully
infiltrated in the financial
industry...

01:15:57.678 --> 01:16:02.891
which I loved and adored,
and had been doing since
I graduated from college.

01:16:02.891 --> 01:16:08.522
And as time passed, I started to
see everything going on in the
financial markets...

01:16:08.564 --> 01:16:11.233
just huge blowups.

01:16:11.275 --> 01:16:17.239
I then, in fact, got my pink
slip, and ended up being laid
off.

01:16:17.281 --> 01:16:20.367
And I often remember my mom
saying like the farm...

01:16:20.409 --> 01:16:22.744
is what saved her parents
during the Depression.

01:16:22.786 --> 01:16:28.417
They also did like crazy stuff
like, you know, sell moonshine,
I guess.

01:16:28.417 --> 01:16:32.337
And you know, like house
different um.. people coming
over from Europe...

01:16:32.379 --> 01:16:35.257
that needed extra housing and
put them up.

01:16:35.257 --> 01:16:38.594
Um.. but always kind of like
thinking of different ways
of uhm..

01:16:38.594 --> 01:16:41.680
being smart and making money
and- and saving their money.

01:16:41.722 --> 01:16:45.934
I worked really hard in finance,
but I'm working a lot harder
now...

01:16:45.934 --> 01:16:50.272
than I ever worked in finance,
you know, for- for nothing,
basically, you know.

01:16:50.314 --> 01:16:53.066
Hopefully to potentially attain
something...

01:16:53.108 --> 01:16:56.945
but it's a return to
intelligence and creativity and,
you know...

01:16:56.945 --> 01:17:01.241
this emergence of young
entrepreneurs, with socially
conscious types of businesses.

01:17:01.283 --> 01:17:06.246
We're all kind of like bartering
with one another of how to get
work done, and how to, you know,

01:17:06.288 --> 01:17:09.041
get a fashion show done
with no capital.

01:17:09.082 --> 01:17:11.877
"Well, this person needs this
exposure, and I need this."

01:17:11.918 --> 01:17:14.087
"And let's like figure out a way
to make this happen."

01:17:14.129 --> 01:17:17.132
So yeah, it's an interesting
time, and it's--

01:17:17.132 --> 01:17:20.761
it's also like something great
to be a part of, I feel.

01:17:29.561 --> 01:17:34.149
Fang, my husband, Fang, uh..

01:17:34.191 --> 01:17:41.239
was so cheap that once he took
the kids and me out...

01:17:41.281 --> 01:17:45.494
for coffee and donuts.

01:17:45.535 --> 01:17:50.374
The kids loved it, because they
had never given blood before.

01:17:54.795 --> 01:17:59.341
What a joke.
That's a beautiful joke.

01:17:59.341 --> 01:18:04.346
In retrospect, it may look like
a great thing that happened...

01:18:04.346 --> 01:18:10.560
that was a necessity to get us
back on the track properly.

01:18:10.602 --> 01:18:12.354
Because after all...

01:18:12.354 --> 01:18:18.360
we, each of us are a part
of human evolution.

01:18:18.360 --> 01:18:21.154
And we take with us technology.

01:18:21.196 --> 01:18:25.367
And we're speeding
into the universe.

01:18:25.409 --> 01:18:31.498
Your experience that you had
coming through those times
sustain you.

01:18:31.540 --> 01:18:38.505
They make you strong, you're
tested in a crucible of a fire.

01:18:38.547 --> 01:18:41.049
The Depression beat people down.

01:18:41.049 --> 01:18:44.344
So there's wonderful and
romantically to say...

01:18:44.386 --> 01:18:47.931
"Look at how these people pulled
themselves up and made something
of themselves."

01:18:47.973 --> 01:18:52.561
But there's also that other half
of the population that couldn't
do that.

01:18:52.561 --> 01:18:57.232
That were so beaten down by the
Depression that it a- affected
their mindset...

01:18:57.232 --> 01:19:01.778
and people around them, and
their families, for- for years
and years to come.

01:19:01.820 --> 01:19:04.823
So unless you have that
courage to keep on...

01:19:04.865 --> 01:19:09.661
and those early days in the
Depression days molded a lot
of people.

01:19:09.703 --> 01:19:14.833
They gave them the courage and
the strength to become somebody.

01:19:14.875 --> 01:19:19.546
I would not say it makes us
stronger. I would say it
makes us free.

01:19:19.588 --> 01:19:22.549
It makes us more confident
when it's all over.

01:19:22.591 --> 01:19:24.760
It makes us confident in our
ability to survive.

01:19:24.760 --> 01:19:28.638
That part of it is true.
But it doesn't make our
beliefs stronger.

01:19:28.680 --> 01:19:30.682
It usually destroys them.

01:19:30.724 --> 01:19:34.603
It doesn't make our family
systems stronger, it usually
injures them.

01:19:34.644 --> 01:19:39.316
It doesn't make our economic
system stronger or our political
system stronger...

01:19:39.357 --> 01:19:40.734
it usually wrecks it.

01:19:40.776 --> 01:19:43.779
So I don't think in those senses
that it made us stronger.

01:19:43.779 --> 01:19:50.494
I think what it did was force us
to think freely and anew about
our lives.

01:19:50.535 --> 01:19:54.581
And the way we're going to judge
truth, the way we communicate to
each other.

01:19:54.623 --> 01:19:57.417
trust and not trust.ling to

01:19:57.459 --> 01:19:59.127
I think it's a moment
of enlightenment.

01:19:59.169 --> 01:20:03.215
Now, it's usually accompanied
by nausea.

01:20:03.256 --> 01:20:05.801
We're so often saved in spite
of ourselves, you know?

01:20:05.842 --> 01:20:08.762
There's- there's something
Arnold Toynbee, a great
historian...

01:20:08.804 --> 01:20:11.473
said that his lifelong study
of-- and he was no mystic.

01:20:11.473 --> 01:20:13.391
He was a very practical man.

01:20:13.433 --> 01:20:17.062
He said his lifelong study
of history had led him
to the conclusion...

01:20:17.103 --> 01:20:21.608
that every time the puddle of
human affairs was about to
congeal completely...

01:20:21.650 --> 01:20:24.277
the finger of God comes down
and stirs it.

01:20:24.319 --> 01:20:26.404
And I thought,
"It seems like that sometimes!"

01:20:26.446 --> 01:20:32.994
One of the beauties of that
period was that though we had no
money of any large--

01:20:32.994 --> 01:20:38.792
except for that small upper five
or six percent of the
population...

01:20:38.834 --> 01:20:43.547
who were jumping out of windows
or committing suicide because
they couldn't take it--

01:20:43.588 --> 01:20:46.174
we had ways to take it.

01:20:46.216 --> 01:20:52.931
And that, again, is the factor
that is a part of American life
and American dream...

01:20:52.973 --> 01:20:57.060
is that we can take it because
it's going to get better!

01:20:58.144 --> 01:21:02.107
( INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC PLAYING )

